window.__NOTE_CONTENT = "<h2 id=\"international-and-regional-human-rights-regimes\">INTERNATIONAL AND REGIONAL HUMAN RIGHTS REGIMES</h2>\n<h2 id=\"part-i\">PART I</h2>\n<h4 id=\"1-0-general-introduction\">1.0 General Introduction:</h4>\n<p>The objective of the course is to study and examine promotion and protection</p>\n<p>of human rights at the international and regional levels. It is arranged in 3 parts.</p>\n<p>Part I deals with a brief introduction to international law. This is because there</p>\n<p>is a need to concisely provide the students with the requisite grounding in the</p>\n<p>principles of international law, including international law of human rights and</p>\n<p>the sources thereof. It is also considered appropriate to highlight the definition</p>\n<p>and concept of human rights although this was undertaken last semester. Part</p>\n<p>II covers the international System, while Part III is devoted to the regional</p>\n<p>systems of human rights.</p>\n<p>1.1 Definition and Concept of Human Rights</p>\n<p>It is my understanding that you have covered this under various courses in this</p>\n<p>programme in the last semester. I do not propose to repeat it here, but it is</p>\n<p>important to point out that human rights have been characterised as those rights</p>\n<p>that society has agreed are fundamental to people everywhere, such as the right</p>\n<p>to life, the right to live without oppression, and the right to equal freedom of</p>\n<p>opportunity.1 They have also been referred to as those rights without which we</p>\n<p>cannot leave as human beings.2 This characterisation leaves a big question. Does</p>\n<p>a human being cease to be human simply because his rights have been violated</p>\n<p>or suspended? This statement may be analysed and discussed particularly in the</p>\n<p>light of the course on Philosophy of human rights.</p>\n<p>Professor Luis Menkin has attempted to define human rights. For definition see,</p>\n<p>George W.K.L.Kasozi, Introduction to the Law of Lesotho: A Basic Text on</p>\n<p>1</p>\n<p>See Questions and Answers: Protecting Human Rights, BBC News, World Edition (Wednesday 28</p>\n<p>November 2001.</p>\n<p>2</p>\n<p>The United Nations formulation - contained in: Lear Levin: Human Rights Questions and Answers, Third</p>\n<p>edition, UNESCO Publishing, 1996 p 4 and 15.</p>\n<p>Law and Judicial Conduct, Vol. 1, Ministry of Justice Human Rights and</p>\n<p>Rehabilitation, 1999, p.15. Professor Menkin is also quoted by the Honourable</p>\n<p>Chief Justice of Uganda, Justice Benjamin J. Odoki, in Creating and Sustaining a</p>\n<p>Culture of Respect for Human Rights, Uganda Christian University Law Review,</p>\n<p>Vol 1. No.1 (Reissue) April 2009, p.5 – 19 at p.5.</p>\n<p>1.2 The Nature and Significance of Human Rights</p>\n<p>This section also has been examined in the previous courses, however it is</p>\n<p>important to look at cases that may help us in this regard. I have so far not yet</p>\n<p>secured an authority in Uganda hence reference may be made to jurisprudence</p>\n<p>formulated by eminent scholars and jurists in the area of human rights and cases</p>\n<p>from Courts else where.</p>\n<p>Let us begin with the remarks the words of the Chief Justice of Japan quoted</p>\n<p>in: Joseph Garang and Others v The Constituent Assembly3. He is on record as</p>\n<h4 id=\"having-said\">having said:</h4>\n<p>“Fundamental human rights were not vested by the state but are</p>\n<p>eternal     and universal institutions common to all mankind and</p>\n<p>antedating the state and    are founded on natural law.4</p>\n<p>It is also useful to consider the words of the Honourable Ismael Mahomed,</p>\n<p>former President of the Court of Appeal for Lesotho, who later became the Chief</p>\n<p>Justice of the Republic of South Africa. He had this to say about human rights</p>\n<p>and their observance by the state in the case Attorney General of Lesotho v.</p>\n<h4 id=\"swissbrough-diamond-mines-pty-ltd-and-another\">Swissbrough Diamond Mines (PTY) Ltd and Another:</h4>\n<p>“…that the fundamental human rights which every citizen in a</p>\n<p>country          is entitled to exercise inhere in him in consequence of</p>\n<p>his humanity and               therefore inalienable; that no government</p>\n<p>however powerful be its            military arsenal, however awesome its</p>\n<p>3</p>\n<p>1997 (8) BCLR p. 1154 (Lesotho Court of Appeal. The relevant part is situated on p. 1160, paragraph D.</p>\n<p>(Butterworths Constitutional Law Reports) (South Africa)</p>\n<p>4</p>\n<p>Case No CS/93/1965. See also Akinola Aguda: Discriminatory Statutory Provisions and Fundamental</p>\n<p>Rights Provisions of the Constitution of Botswana, Lesotho and Swaziland, SALJ Vol 89, 1972 p 299.</p>\n<p>Aguda is a former Chief Justice of Botswana and former Judge of the Western State of Nigeria.</p>\n<p>police power indeed even however                     popular be its actual or</p>\n<p>perceived support among the populace at any given time in its history,</p>\n<p>dare be permitted to invade fundamental rights -           however humble,</p>\n<p>however impotent, be the victim of such transgression;                 that the</p>\n<p>courts will fiercely protect its citizens against the invasion of his</p>\n<p>rights; that the law has the sovereignty and life beyond the life of the rulers</p>\n<p>in a country who come and go”.</p>\n<p>Finally, let us hear what another judge - Ramodibedi J, had to say about the</p>\n<p>what should be done about violation of human rights in the case of Molapo v</p>\n<h4 id=\"dpp5-he-said\">DPP5. He said:</h4>\n<p>“In my judgment any violation of human rights regardless of the</p>\n<p>degree         thereof deserves to be stamped out in a just democratic</p>\n<p>society that prides itself with a bill of rights entrenched in the</p>\n<p>Constitution such as</p>\n<p>Lesotho</p>\n<p>is”</p>\n<p>1.3 The Nature and Definition of International Law and International</p>\n<p>Human Rights Law.</p>\n<p>Although this course does not specifically focus on Public International Law</p>\n<p>proper, but rather on International and Regional Human Rights Regimes, it is</p>\n<p>important to look at what International Law means, because International</p>\n<p>Human Rights Law is an offshoot of Public International Law. It is anticipated</p>\n<p>that next academic year this will be taught as a separate course in the first</p>\n<p>Semester.</p>\n<p>A conservative definition of International Law views international law as being:</p>\n<p>“A body of rules governing the relations between states”</p>\n<p>This definition is however limited, because it only focuses on state and not</p>\n<p>individuals and international organisations.</p>\n<h4 id=\"starke-an-eminent-scholar-of-international-law-defines-international-law-as\">Starke, an eminent scholar of International Law defines International Law as:</h4>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>1997 (8) BCLR p 1154 at 1160 paragraph D.</p>\n<p>“That body of law which is composed for the greater part of the</p>\n<p>principles           and rules of conduct which states feel bound to observe,</p>\n<p>and therefore do               commonly observe in their relation with each</p>\n<h4 id=\"other-and-which-include-also\">other, and which include            also:</h4>\n<p>(a) the rules of law relating to the functioning of international</p>\n<p>organis- ations or organizations, their relations with each other</p>\n<p>an, and their relations with states and individuals; and</p>\n<p>(b)   certain rules of law relating to individuals and non state</p>\n<p>entities              which are the concern of the international</p>\n<p>community”</p>\n<p>It is now established in international law and also as seen in the words of Starke</p>\n<p>that individuals are now subjects of international law as enunciated in the famous</p>\n<p>Reparations for Injuries Suffered in the Service of the United Nations case 6. In</p>\n<p>this case the United Nations had sought the opinion of the International Court</p>\n<p>of Justice, after the United Nations truce negotiator, one</p>\n<p>Count Bernadotte, a Swedish national, was killed by terrorists on 17th</p>\n<p>September 1948, in the new city of Jerusalem, which was in Israeli possession.</p>\n<p>The International Court of Justice gave its advisory opinion, stating inter alia that:</p>\n<p>“Accordingly the court has come to the conclusion that the</p>\n<p>organization is                  an international person. That is not the same</p>\n<p>thing as saying that it is a              state, which it certainly is not, or that</p>\n<p>its legal personality and rights and               duties are the same as those</p>\n<p>of the state. Still less is it the same thing as              saying it is a super</p>\n<p>state…What it does mean is that it is a subject of                   international</p>\n<p>law capable of possessing international rights and duties                 and that</p>\n<p>it has capacity to maintain its rights by bringing an international</p>\n<p>claim.”</p>\n<p>International law has since 1949 evolved and it is now accepted that individuals</p>\n<p>have international legal personality. What this means is that individuals are may</p>\n<p>6</p>\n<p>See: ICJ Reports (1949) p 174. This case is also documented in DJ Harris: Cases and Materials on</p>\n<p>International Law, Sweet and Maxwell, 1983, Third Edition p 114.</p>\n<p>hold other international personalities including states accountable at the</p>\n<p>international level.</p>\n<p>Although it is stated that international human rights law is a branch of</p>\n<p>international law, it is important to define it. International human rights law</p>\n<p>is that branch of international law, which defines, elaborates, provides</p>\n<p>relevant principles and governs the promotion and protection of human</p>\n<p>rights at the international and regional levels. This is what we will be studying</p>\n<p>in this course.</p>\n<p>Now that we have ascertained what international law is and the subjects thereof,</p>\n<p>we may turn to look at the sources of this law.</p>\n<p>1.4 Nature and Sources of International Law and International Human</p>\n<p>Rights Law</p>\n<p>The nature and sources of International law are documented in Article 38</p>\n<p>paragraph 1 of the Statute of the International Court of Justice. This Statute is</p>\n<h4 id=\"part-of-the-united-nations-charter-it-states-as-follows\">part of the United Nations Charter. It states as follows:</h4>\n<p>“The court, whose function is to decide in accordance with</p>\n<h4 id=\"international-law-such-disputes-as-are-submitted-to-it-shall-apply\">international       law such disputes as are submitted to it, shall apply:</h4>\n<p>(a) International   conventions, whether general or particular,</p>\n<p>establishing      rules expressly recognized by contesting states;</p>\n<p>(b) International customs as evidence of general practice accepted as</p>\n<p>law;</p>\n<p>(c) the general principles of law recognized by civilized nations;</p>\n<p>(d) subject to the principles of Article 59, judicial decisions and</p>\n<p>teaching of most highly qualified publicists of the various nations</p>\n<p>as subsidiary means for the determination of rules of law.</p>\n<p>In short the Article recognizes 5 major sources of international law, including</p>\n<p>international human rights law, namely: Conventions/Treaties, International</p>\n<p>Custom, General Principles of Law Recognised by Civilised</p>\n<p>States, Judicial Decisions and Teachings of Most Highly Qualified</p>\n<p>Publicists.</p>\n<p>In addition to the above there is the sixth source, which is the Resolutions of the</p>\n<p>United Nations General Assembly. This is subject to certain conditions as will</p>\n<p>be seen later.</p>\n<p>1.4.1 Treaties/Conventions</p>\n<p>Treaties are a very important source of international law generally and human</p>\n<p>rights law in particular. It is however important to point out that that there are 2</p>\n<p>types of treaties. These are 1 - Law Making Treaties also known as Traite’s</p>\n<p>lois and 2 - The Ordinary Contractual Treaties or Traite’s contracts.</p>\n<p>Generally speaking law making treaties are considered to lay down rules binding</p>\n<p>a large number of states, for example The Hague Conventions andf variuos</p>\n<p>Conventions concluded under the auspices of the United Nations - such as the</p>\n<p>International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR) The International</p>\n<p>Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (ICESCR) etc.</p>\n<p>On the other hand Ordinary Contractual Treaties are in essence bilateral or</p>\n<p>treaties between a few parties, only directed to a special object affecting the</p>\n<p>interest of the signatories.</p>\n<p>As far as the Law Making Treaties are concerned, their provisions are directly a</p>\n<p>source of international law, whereas this is not the case with Ordinary</p>\n<p>Contractual Treaties. A good example here is the treaty between Uganda, The</p>\n<p>Sudan and Egypt on the sharing of the waters of river Nile.</p>\n<p>It is a condition precedent that for a Law Making Treaty to be a source</p>\n<p>international law, it must have entered in force, that is it has been ratified by the</p>\n<p>requisite number of states parties.</p>\n<p>Oppenheimer7 coined a useful definition of law making treaties. He says that</p>\n<h4 id=\"law-making-treaties-are\">Law Making Treaties are:</h4>\n<p>“…Such treaties…as stipulate new general rules for future</p>\n<p>international             conduct or confirm, define, or abolish existing</p>\n<p>customary or conventional         rules of a general character.”</p>\n<h4 id=\"brierly8-looks-at-law-making-treaties-as\">Brierly8 looks at Law Making Treaties as:</h4>\n<p>“Those which the a large number of of states have concluded for</p>\n<p>the           purpose of either declaring their understanding of what the</p>\n<p>law is on a        particular subject, or of laying down rules of a general</p>\n<p>rule for the           future conduct or of creating some international</p>\n<p>institution.”</p>\n<p>Finally, it should be pointed out that as far as Law Making Treaties are concerned,</p>\n<p>they should be adopted by almost all the great states of the world as pointed out</p>\n<h4 id=\"by-sir-frederick-pollock9-when-he-says\">by Sir Frederick Pollock9 when he says:</h4>\n<p>“There ids no doubt that, when all or most of the great powers</p>\n<p>have            deliberately agreed to certain rules of general application,</p>\n<p>the rules           approved by them have very great weight in practice</p>\n<p>even among             those states which have never expressly consented</p>\n<p>to them. It is            hardly too much to say that declarations of this</p>\n<p>kind may be expected               in the absence of prompt and efficient</p>\n<p>dissent by some power of the                   first rank, to be part of the</p>\n<p>universally received law of nations within a               moderate time.”</p>\n<p>A state which ratifies a Law Making Treaty may make a reservation to any</p>\n<p>treaty, but such a reservation must be compatible with the treaty. What this</p>\n<p>means is that a state may not make a reservation which goes to the root of the</p>\n<p>treaty and renders is irrelevant. For example if the state ratifies The Convention</p>\n<p>Against Torture (CAT), such a state must not make a reservation to the effect</p>\n<p>that .. the treatment meted out to criminals in order to extract information</p>\n<p>7</p>\n<p>See ……………</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>See…..</p>\n<p>9</p>\n<p>See Columbia Law Review 1902.</p>\n<p>from them or the harsh punishment ordered by the courts of the land for</p>\n<p>murders or terrorists will not be brought into question.</p>\n<p>1.4.2 International Custom</p>\n<p>What does International Custom mean and how does it come about? According</p>\n<p>to George Scelle10 international custom results from similar and repeated acts</p>\n<p>by states. The acts must be repeated with a conscious conviction of the parties</p>\n<p>that they are acting in conformity with law. There are 2 important factors in the</p>\n<h4 id=\"formation-of-international-custom-these-are\">formation of international custom. These are:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-there-must-be-a-material-fact-the-repetition-of-similar-facts-by-the-state\">1. There must be a material fact (The repetition of similar facts by the state)</h3>\n<p>2 . There must be a psychological element usually referred to as opino juris sive</p>\n<p>necessitatis - That is the feeling on the part of the states that in acting as they</p>\n<p>do, they are fulfilling a legal obligation.</p>\n<p>These 2 factors contribute to what is called state practice, which is emanate</p>\n<p>from the state’s international organs - including the Head of State and the</p>\n<p>Minister of Foreign Affairs on the general policy and of the government.</p>\n<p>According to D’Amato11, state practice state practice should be restricted to the</p>\n<p>physical acts of the state. Claims are not enough. A claim is not an act although</p>\n<p>such a claim may articulate a legal norm. This is because claims and statements</p>\n<p>by states are likely to conflict with one another, yet physical acts do not suffer</p>\n<p>from this defect.</p>\n<p>1.4.3 Judicial Decisions</p>\n<p>The decisions of the International Court of Justice are a useful source of</p>\n<p>international law. These decisions are authoritative. The International Court of</p>\n<p>Justice is constituted by judges and lawyers who are well trained and are from</p>\n<p>various legal jurisdictions. These learned men bring to the court vast experience.</p>\n<p>Their judgments are therefore solid and well grounded, hence authoritative. This</p>\n<p>source is considered by Article 38 of the International Court of Justice statute as</p>\n<p>a subsidiary source of law.</p>\n<p>10</p>\n<p>French Scholar who wrote his works around 1943. See…………</p>\n<p>11</p>\n<p>See…………</p>\n<p>1.4.4 General Principles of law recognized by civilized states</p>\n<p>There are some jurists or legal philosophers such as Verdross, who argue that</p>\n<p>Article 38 of the ICJ Statute has the effect of incorporating natural law in</p>\n<p>international law. They even claim that positive rules of international law are</p>\n<p>invalid if they conflict with natural law.</p>\n<p>There are those on the extreme side such as Guggenheim and Tunkin, who</p>\n<p>propound the view that paragraph (C) of article 38 of the ICJ adds nothing to</p>\n<p>what is already covered by treaty and custom. They argue that the general</p>\n<p>principles of national law are part of international law only to the extent that they</p>\n<p>have been adopted by states in treaties or in state practice.</p>\n<p>There is also another group of philosophers, who may be considered to be</p>\n<p>middle of the road. They take the position that general principles recognised in</p>\n<p>national law form part of the pool of principles which an international judge is</p>\n<p>authorised by Article 38 to apply in an international dispute, if their application</p>\n<p>appears to be relevant and appropriate in the context of the inter state relations.</p>\n<p>The main areas where these principles have been applied are the general</p>\n<p>principles of legal liability and reparation for breaches of international obligations</p>\n<p>or administration of justice. An example of this is found in the Chorzow Factory</p>\n<p>Case, where the Permanent Court (predecessor to the International Court of</p>\n<p>Justice) described the principle, that a party can not take advantage of its own</p>\n<p>wrong, as a principle accepted in the jurisprudence of international arbitration,</p>\n<p>as well as by domestic or municipal courts. Later in the above-mentioned case</p>\n<p>the court observed that it is a general conception of law hat every violation of an</p>\n<p>agreement involves an obligation to make reparations. The court went further</p>\n<p>to say that as far as the administration of justice is concerned there are a number</p>\n<p>of references to the general principles of law in connection with the question of</p>\n<p>jurisdiction, procedure, evidence or other aspects of the judicial process.</p>\n<p>1.4.5 Teachings of the most highly qualified publicists of the various</p>\n<p>nations.</p>\n<p>The teachings of most highly qualified publicists or writers have been identified</p>\n<p>by article 38 of the ICJ as a subsidiary source of international law. There are some</p>\n<p>writers who have been acknowledged as authoritative in the sphere of</p>\n<p>international law. For example Grotious, a Dutch national is considered the</p>\n<p>father of the law of nations. Vattel, who at the beginning of the 19th Century was</p>\n<p>considered a great authority and many scholars at the time relied on his works.</p>\n<p>There are others such as Oppenheimer, Stake etc. In the modern times scholars</p>\n<p>such as Akehurst, Brownlie, Harris, Sieghart are authoritative as far as</p>\n<p>international law is concerned.</p>\n<p>1.4.6 Resolutions of the UN General Assembly</p>\n<p>The resolutions of the General Assembly are not binding per se and do not</p>\n<p>impose any obligation on member states, notwithstanding the provision laid</p>\n<p>down in Article 25 of the Charter of the United Nations. General Assembly</p>\n<p>resolutions are not legally binding except when addressed to the subsidiary</p>\n<p>organs of the Secretariat.</p>\n<p>Akehurst argues that most resolutions have nothing to do with international law,</p>\n<p>but that even when they do, they may simply be recommendatory in nature and</p>\n<p>as such, the text of a resolution can not be construed to represent the existing</p>\n<p>law. He notes further that the value of such a resolution varies in proportion to</p>\n<p>the number of states voting for it. If for example many states vote against it, its</p>\n<p>value as evidence of customary law is accordingly reduced.</p>\n<p>1.5. A Brief Introduction to Relevant Principles and Doctrines in</p>\n<p>International Law.</p>\n<p>(a) Sovereignty</p>\n<p>States in the conduct of their affairs have an area, which is exclusively within their</p>\n<p>jurisdiction. The principle of sovereignty provides that other states, individuals</p>\n<p>or organisations must not interfere.</p>\n<p>Sovereignty is reinforced by Article 2 paragraph 7 of the United Nations Charter.</p>\n<h4 id=\"i-states-as-follows\">I states as follows:</h4>\n<p>“Nothing contained in the present Charter shall authorise</p>\n<p>the United               Nations to intervene in matters which are</p>\n<p>essentially within the           domestic jurisdiction of any state or</p>\n<p>shall require the members                 to submit such matters to</p>\n<p>settlement under the present Charter, but               this principle</p>\n<p>shall not prejudice the application of enforcement          measures</p>\n<p>under chapter VII Article 39 - 51.</p>\n<p>Does sovereignty provide a state, which violates human rights with a shield? Can</p>\n<p>such a state dismiss international intervention simply because that state feels that</p>\n<p>such matters are within its domestic jurisdiction? Human rights today are not</p>\n<p>matters of domestic jurisdiction. Human rights violations are a business of every</p>\n<p>one, every where. States can not be permitted to shield behind the principle or</p>\n<p>doctrine of state sovereignty. When there are massive violations of human rights</p>\n<p>in any given country there is bound to be tension in the whole world. This</p>\n<p>violation becomes the concern of the international community, hence the need</p>\n<p>to intervene.</p>\n<p>The principle of sovereignty does not entitle a state to ill treat aliens on its</p>\n<p>territory. Aliens too must be treated well. If aliens are expelled or treated unjustly</p>\n<p>and or unlawfully, other states may denounce such a move. This was the case in</p>\n<p>the 1970s, when the former President of Uganda, Field Marshall Idi Amin</p>\n<p>Dada expelled the British Asians, including Ugandan Asians. In the 1980 Nigeria</p>\n<p>expelled Ghanaians from her territory. All these were condemned by the</p>\n<p>international community. The International Minimum Standards for the</p>\n<p>Treatment of Aliens must be observed.</p>\n<p>(b) Derogation</p>\n<p>This is a provision in a treaty which permits a state party by unilateral declaration</p>\n<p>in certain prescribed circumstances not to adhere to the provisions of a human</p>\n<p>rights treaty. This is usually during a state of emergency. It is important to point</p>\n<p>out that derogation must be expressly provided for e.g Article 4 paragraph (1) of</p>\n<p>the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, which provides as</p>\n<h4 id=\"follows\">follows:</h4>\n<p>“In time of public emergency which threatens the life of a nation</p>\n<p>and the existence of which is officially proclaimed, the state parties</p>\n<p>to the present covenant may take measures derogating from their</p>\n<p>obligations under the present covenant may take measures derogating</p>\n<p>from their obligations under the present covenant to the extent strictly</p>\n<p>required by the exigencies of the situation, provided that such measures</p>\n<p>are not inconsistent with their obligation under international law and do</p>\n<p>not involve discrimination solely on the ground of race, colour, sex,</p>\n<p>language, religion, or social origin.”</p>\n<p>(2) No derogation from Article 6,7,8 (paragraphs 1 and 2,</p>\n<p>11,15,16,               and 18 may be made under this provision Any</p>\n<p>state party to the            present covenant availing itself of the right</p>\n<p>of derogation shall            immediately inform the other state parties</p>\n<p>to the present covenant                 through the intermediary of the</p>\n<p>Secretary General of the U.N of the              provisions from which</p>\n<p>it has derogated and of the reasons by which it             was actuated.</p>\n<p>A further communication shall be made through the same</p>\n<p>intermediary, on the date on which it terminates such derogation.</p>\n<p>Similar provisions are found in the European Human Rights Convention -</p>\n<p>Article 15, Economic Social and Cultural Rights Article 30, American Convention</p>\n<p>of Human Rights Article 27. What about the African Charter on Human and</p>\n<p>People’s Rights?</p>\n<p>(c) Denunciation</p>\n<p>A treaty, like a domestic contract may come to an end through many ways. It</p>\n<p>may come to an end as a result of the consent of the parties, when there is</p>\n<p>material breach by one of the parties, when there is a supervening impossibility</p>\n<p>of performance, or when there is a fundamental change of circumstances.</p>\n<p>Denunciation however is one of the ways a treaty may come to an end.</p>\n<p>Denunciation is the procedure by which a state party to a treaty may unilaterally</p>\n<p>abrogate its obligation under the treaty in question. Denunciation is usually</p>\n<p>conveyed by written notification to the concerned parties, or to the central</p>\n<p>depository.</p>\n<p>Where a treaty does not expressly provide for denunciation, a state party can not</p>\n<p>abrogate its obligations under the treaty, unless a right of denunciation is implied</p>\n<p>by the nature of the treaty.</p>\n<p>An example of denunciation is provided for under Article 12 (1) of the Optional</p>\n<p>Protocol to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights. It provides</p>\n<h4 id=\"as-follows\">as follows:</h4>\n<p>“Any state party may denounce the present protocol at any</p>\n<p>time                by written notification addressed to the Secretary</p>\n<p>General               of the United Nations. Denunciation shall take</p>\n<p>effect three months          after the date of receipt of the notification</p>\n<p>by the Secretary General.”</p>\n<p>(2) Denunciation shall be without prejudice to the continued</p>\n<p>appli            cation of the provisions of the present protocol to any</p>\n<p>communication         submitted under Article 2 before the effective date</p>\n<p>of denunciation”</p>\n<p>Similar provisions are found in the European Convention on Human Rights-</p>\n<p>Article 65 (1 – 4), International Covenant on Social, Economic and Cultural</p>\n<p>Rights Article 37 (1-3, The American Convention on Human Rights Article 78</p>\n<p>(1 – 2. What about the African Charter?</p>\n<p>1.5.2 Monism and Dualism</p>\n<p>When we discuss the issue of monism and dualism, we are talking about</p>\n<p>international law and municipal or domestic law and whether there are 2 separate</p>\n<p>legal orders or regimes existing independent of each other.</p>\n<p>There is a school of thought, which is of the view that the two systems are in</p>\n<p>essence one. This school of thought is known as the monist school. On the other</p>\n<p>hand there is another school of thought, which is of the view that there are 2</p>\n<p>legal systems, which exist side by side and that neither is superior nor inferior to</p>\n<p>the other.</p>\n<p>This school further notes that these 2 systems govern two jurisdictions, namely</p>\n<p>the international jurisdiction and the domestic or municipal or national</p>\n<p>jurisdiction.</p>\n<p>The dualist school argues that in the event of conflict between these systems</p>\n<p>there are clearly established rules, which help to resolve the conflict, namely the</p>\n<p>rules of Private International Law, or what is some times referred to as Conflict</p>\n<p>of Laws</p>\n<p>.</p>\n<p>1.5.3 Inalienability</p>\n<p>International law and international human rights law have borrowed this concept</p>\n<p>from ethics and philosophy. To alienate means to take away or give away.</p>\n<p>Fundamental human rights in essence should not be taken away or be given up.</p>\n<p>They inhere in the human person, eg the right to life. There are other rights,</p>\n<p>which may not be necessarily referred to fundamental human rights, but rather</p>\n<p>as right. These may be renounced, for example the right to marry.</p>\n<p>As far as international human rights law is concerned, when we talk about</p>\n<p>inalienability of human rights we are focusing on fundamental human rights.</p>\n<p>1.5.4 Indivisibility, Interconnectedness and Interrelated</p>\n<p>Although in the international human rights system we talk about civil and</p>\n<p>political rights on the one hand and economic, social and cultural rights on the</p>\n<p>other, and although these rights are protected by 2 international human rights</p>\n<p>instruments, human rights are a distinct whole, hence they are said to be</p>\n<p>indivisible. They are interconnected and interrelated. For example the right to</p>\n<p>life is connected to the right to food, shelter and a clean environment.</p>\n<p>These rights should be treated in the same way. Experience has shown that there</p>\n<p>is a tendency to elevate civil and political rights and to accord less importance to</p>\n<p>the economic, social and cultural rights.</p>\n<p>The reason for this is the ideological divide, which had, until a few years ago</p>\n<p>polarized the world into two blocks, namely the Western and Eastern blocks.</p>\n<p>The Western block countries tended to concentrate and emphasise the civil and</p>\n<p>political rights, while the Eastern block dwelled on the economic, social and</p>\n<p>cultural rights.</p>\n<p>A good example may be provided by an illustration by a cartoonist, with a caption, which posed</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-following-questions\">the following questions:</h4>\n<p>When and where do human rights begin? Do they begin at the police station or after breakfast?</p>\n<p>1.5.5 Universalism and Relativism</p>\n<p>Scholars have come up with explanations regarding the character of human</p>\n<p>rights. Those who advocate the universality of human rights claim that rights</p>\n<p>such as equal protection, physical security, freedom of speech, freedom of</p>\n<p>religion and association are and must be the same every where. Reference to</p>\n<p>every-where is meant to mean all over the world.</p>\n<p>The proponent of this view disregard culture as being relevant in the</p>\n<p>interpretation and realization of human rights. What they seem to overlook is</p>\n<p>that some basic rights, such as the right to a fair trial in criminal proceedings,</p>\n<p>permit culturally influenced forms of implementation. For example it is not</p>\n<p>imperative that all states of the world must follow the jury trial as is the case in</p>\n<p>United Kingdom and United States of America, for it to be said that the all states</p>\n<p>of the world observe fair trial in their criminal justice systems. What about the</p>\n<p>right to political participation? Must it be the same every-where? I do not have</p>\n<p>the answer. It would be useful for the scholars answer this question</p>\n<p>Cultural relativists argue that most or some rights and rules about morality are</p>\n<p>embedded or enclosed in culture. Culture is used in this context in a wider sense</p>\n<p>beyond indigenous traditions and customary practices. It includes political and</p>\n<p>religious ideologies as well as institutional structures.</p>\n<p>The cultural relativists are also of the view that the world contains diversity in</p>\n<p>views about right and wrong, that is linked to the diverse underlying cultures.</p>\n<p>They also argue that no culture is or state whether or not in the guise of enforcing</p>\n<p>human rights, is justified in attempting to impose on other cultures or states,</p>\n<p>what must be understood to be ideas associated particularly with it.</p>\n<p>Cultural relativists assume that there is no culture whose values and customs</p>\n<p>dominate in a moral sense. They maintain that every culture has an equal right to</p>\n<p>be different, distinct and unique.</p>\n<p>In conclusion cultural relativists advance the argument that there are different</p>\n<p>perceptions of human rights by different societies or communities, such that</p>\n<p>what may constitute violation of human rights in one culture may not be</p>\n<p>considered as violation of human rights in another culture. The main criticism</p>\n<p>against the relativist school of thought is that there are some practices, which</p>\n<p>may be performed in the name of culture, yet these practices are contrary to</p>\n<p>international human rights standards. In this regard one may point to</p>\n<p>circumcision of women or what has now been referred to as Female Genital</p>\n<p>Mutilation. Another criticism is that cultural relativists seem to believe that</p>\n<p>culture is static. These issues may be further explored by the students on their</p>\n<p>own.12</p>\n<p>1.5.6 Margin of Appreciation</p>\n<p>The doctrine of Margin of Appreciation has been mainly applied in interpreting</p>\n<p>the European Convention of Human Rights as we will see later. The doctrine</p>\n<p>primarily seeks to restrain courts when reviewing executive actions in order not</p>\n<p>to upset the distribution of powers within the legal and constitutional system,</p>\n<p>especially where an issue at stake falls within the core responsibility of the</p>\n<p>executive organ and requires a discretion.</p>\n<p>12</p>\n<p>For further reading see: Lone Lindholt: Questioning the Universality of Human Rights: The African</p>\n<p>Charter on Human and People’s Rights Botswana, Malawi and Mozambique, Sage … 19…, p24 – 25; See</p>\n<p>Karin Poulsen: Human Rights and Cultural Change………. P6; See also George W.K.L.Kasozi:</p>\n<p>Implementation of Human Rights Standards: A Study of Lesotho’s Laws with the International Covenant</p>\n<p>on Civil and Political Rights 1966, with Particular Reference to Gender Based Discrimination, Research</p>\n<p>Partnership, Danish Centre for Human Rights, 2001, p 52 – 54.</p>\n<p>The Sunday Times Case 13 is a useful example. In this case, following the</p>\n<p>thalidomide tragedy, many parents sued the distillers, the British manufacturers</p>\n<p>for negligence in 1972. While negotiations to settle the claims were pending, the</p>\n<p>Sunday Times planned the publication of an article which reviewed the evidence</p>\n<p>on the question whether distillers had been negligent. The Attorney General</p>\n<p>obtained an injunction from the Divisional court, preventing the publication of</p>\n<p>the articles on the ground of contempt. The injunction was discharged by the</p>\n<p>Court of Appeal, but restored by the unanimous decision of the House of Lords.</p>\n<p>Sunday Times then appealed to the European Court of Human Rights, which</p>\n<p>reversed the decision of the House of Lords. The European Court of Human</p>\n<p>Rights held that the injunction contravened section 10 of European Convention</p>\n<p>of Human Rights (Freedom of Expression). It was further held that although the</p>\n<p>restriction against publication of evidence by the Sunday Times was prescribed</p>\n<p>by law 14 and imposed for a legitimate purpose, it was not necessary in a</p>\n<p>democratic society for the maintenance of the authority of the judiciary. (It was</p>\n<p>11 votes to 1.</p>\n<p>13</p>\n<p>See …..</p>\n<p>14</p>\n<p>There are 2 requirements here. 1. Law must be adequately accessible. 2. Law ought to be formulated with</p>\n<p>sufficient precission.</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-11\">PART 11</h2>\n<h2 id=\"2-0-the-international-human-rights-system\">2.0 THE INTERNATIONAL HUMAN RIGHTS SYSTEM</h2>\n<p>There is no universal Parliament, which puts in place the legal framework or</p>\n<p>legislates for the global community, yet it is common cause that there is an</p>\n<p>international regime, which defines, promotes and protects human rights at the</p>\n<p>international level. How is this regime put in place?</p>\n<p>According to Professor Sieghart 15 , the modern international law making</p>\n<p>procedure begins with the adoption of a declaration or a resolution by a major</p>\n<p>organ of an inter-governmental organization such as the General Assembly of</p>\n<p>the United Nations. An example of a declaration is the Universal Declaration of</p>\n<p>Human Rights 1948.</p>\n<p>A declaration or resolution passed by such a body does not by itself establish or</p>\n<p>create a new international law. However, over a period of time such a declaration</p>\n<p>or resolution may be cited as evidence of the consistent practice of states and</p>\n<p>thereby provide the necessary consent for the development of international law.</p>\n<p>(State Practice)</p>\n<p>Following such a declaration or resolution steps may be taken to convert its</p>\n<p>principles into the form of a detailed and binding treaty, negotiated in the same</p>\n<p>body (though sometimes in a different inter-governmental forum).</p>\n<p>When such a treaty enters into force, it immediately creates specific obligations</p>\n<p>in international law for those states, which have ratified the treaty in question.</p>\n<p>An appropriate example here is the ICCPR.</p>\n<p>Global or international and regional human rights treaties have 4 common</p>\n<h4 id=\"characteristics-these-are\">characteristics. These are:</h4>\n<p>15</p>\n<p>See Sieghart, Paul: International Law of Human Rights, Clarendon Press, 19…. P …….</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-they-specify-the-particular-obligations-of-all-their-states-parties-in-respect\">1. They specify the particular obligations of all their states parties in respect</h3>\n<p>in respect of certain human rights and fundamental freedoms of all people</p>\n<p>within their respective territories and subject to their jurisdictions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-they-contain-general-provisions-applying-to-the-protection-or-realization\">2. They contain general provisions applying to the protection or realization</h3>\n<p>of those rights.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-they-define-and-circumscribe-the-rights-and-freedoms-concerned-and\">3. They define and circumscribe the rights and freedoms concerned, and</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-they-establish-institutions-and-procedures-for-the-international\">4. They establish institutions and procedures for the international</h3>\n<p>supervision, interpretation and application of the substantive provisions.</p>\n<p>Let us now focus our attention on the to the international regime.</p>\n<p>2.1 Founding of The United Nations</p>\n<p>In 1945 delegates from 50 countries met in San Francisco, in an international</p>\n<p>forum, which was intended to prepare the United Nations Charter. These</p>\n<p>delegates worked on the proposals that had been forwarded earlier on in 1944,</p>\n<p>by representatives of China, the Soviet Union, United Kingdom and United</p>\n<p>States.</p>\n<p>2.2 The Charter of The United Nations</p>\n<p>The above mentioned international conference came up with a Charter, which</p>\n<p>was extensively negotiated and finally signed in San Francisco, USA, on 26th June,</p>\n<h3 id=\"1945-it-was-called-the-united-nations-charter-the-name-united-nations-was\">1945. It was called the United Nations Charter. The name United Nations was</h3>\n<p>first used by the United States President Franklin Roosvelt. Many Articles of</p>\n<p>this Charter have been amended since 17 December 1963. These include Articles</p>\n<p>23, 27, 61.</p>\n<p>The United Nations was preceded by an organisation known as the League of</p>\n<p>Nations, which was founded in 1919, during the First World War under the</p>\n<p>Treaty of Versailles. The main objective of the League of Nations was to achieve</p>\n<p>peace and security. The League of Nations ceased to exist after it failed to</p>\n<p>prevent the Second World War.</p>\n<p>2.3 The Purposes of the United Nations</p>\n<p>The purposes of the United Nations are detailed in Article 1 of the Charter of</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-united-nations-which-states-as-follows\">the United Nations which states as follows:</h4>\n<p>(1) To maintain international peace and security and to this end to take</p>\n<p>effective collective measures for the prevention and removal of threats to</p>\n<p>peace and for the suppression of acts of aggression or breaches of peace and</p>\n<p>to bring about conformity with principles of justice and international law,</p>\n<p>adjustment or settlement of international disputes or situations which might</p>\n<p>lead to a breach of peace.</p>\n<p>(2) To develop friendly relations among nations based on respect for the</p>\n<p>principle of equal rights and self-determination of peoples, and to take</p>\n<p>appropriate measures to strengthen universal peace.</p>\n<p>(3) To achieve international cooperation in solving international problems of an</p>\n<p>economic, social, cultural or humanitarian character, and in promoting and</p>\n<p>encouraging respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms for all</p>\n<p>without distinction as to race, sex, language, or religion; and</p>\n<p>(4) To be a centre for harmonising the actions of nations in the attainment of</p>\n<p>these common ends.</p>\n<p>Although the United Nations Charter does not specifically mention substantive</p>\n<p>rights, it is very important in providing for promotion and protection of human</p>\n<p>rights as seen in paragraph 3 above. This is also documented in Articles 55 and</p>\n<p>56 of the Charter, whereby all member states of the UN promise to take joint</p>\n<p>and separate action to achieve universal respect for fundamental freedoms for</p>\n<p>all.</p>\n<p>For a detailed discussion of the milestones in the United Nations History and</p>\n<p>Achievements see www.un.org. The web-site documents 50 achievements in</p>\n<p>various areas including promoting human rights generally, promoting women’s</p>\n<p>rights and promoting worker’s rights.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-4-the-structure-of-the-united-nations\">2. 4 The Structure of the United Nations</h3>\n<p>Article 7 of the United Nations Charter establishes six principal organs. These</p>\n<p>are: The General Assembly, The Security Council, The Trusteeship Council, The</p>\n<p>International Court of Justice, and The Secretariat.</p>\n<p>The United Nations entire body or family as it is some times referred to is much</p>\n<p>larger. It has 15 agencies and several programmes. We will look at some of these</p>\n<p>programmes, particularly with emphasis on their involvement in the promotion</p>\n<p>and protection of human rights.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 of article 7 provides for formulation of such subsidiary organs of</p>\n<p>the United Nations as may be found to be necessary.</p>\n<p>2.4 .1 (a) The General Assembly (Composition)</p>\n<p>Membership is open to all members of the United Nations. Each member state</p>\n<p>is entitled to have not more than 5 representatives in the General Assembly. For</p>\n<p>details see Article 9 of the United Nations Charter.</p>\n<p>There are six major Committees through which General Assembly works. These</p>\n<h4 id=\"are\">are:</h4>\n<p>•   First Committee: Disarmament and International Security</p>\n<p>•   Second Committee: Economic and Financial</p>\n<p>•   Third Committee: Social Humanitarian and Cultural</p>\n<p>•   Fourth Committee: Special Political and Decolonisation</p>\n<p>•   Fifth Committee: Administration and Budgetary</p>\n<p>•   Sixth Committee: Legal</p>\n<p>2.4.1 (b) Functions and Powers of the General Assembly</p>\n<p>The General Assembly may discuss any question or issue within the ambit of the</p>\n<p>United Nations Charter or matters pertaining to the powers and function of any</p>\n<p>organs provided for in the Charter, except where the question at hand is a subject</p>\n<p>matter of discussion by or before the Security</p>\n<p>Council.      The General        Assembly is          also empowered to</p>\n<p>make recommendations to the members of United Nations and the Security</p>\n<p>Council. See Article 10</p>\n<p>Article 11 (1) provides for consideration by the General Assembly of the general</p>\n<p>principles of co-operation in the maintenance of international peace and security,</p>\n<p>including the principles governing disarmament and regulation of armaments,</p>\n<p>and make recommendations with regard to such principles to the members of</p>\n<p>the United Nations or Security Council.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 of article 11 empowers the General Assembly to discuss any</p>\n<p>question pertaining to the maintenance of peace and security forwarded to the</p>\n<p>General Assembly by any member of the United Nations or Security Council, or</p>\n<p>any nation, which is not a member of the United Nations in accordance with</p>\n<p>Article 35 paragraph 2.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3 of Article 11 permits the General Assembly to draw to the attention</p>\n<p>of the Security Council any situation likely to jeopardize or endanger international</p>\n<p>peace and security.</p>\n<p>As alluded to in Article 10, Article 12 paragraph 1 specifically prohibits the</p>\n<p>General Assembly to make any recommendation to the Security Council, if the</p>\n<p>Security Council is seized with any dispute or situation, unless the Security</p>\n<p>Council so requests.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 of Article 12 authorises the Secretary General, with the consent of</p>\n<p>the Security Council, to notify the General Assembly at each session of any</p>\n<p>matters relative to the maintenance of international peace and security, which are</p>\n<p>being dealt with the Security Council. The Secretary General is further required</p>\n<p>to notify the General Assembly, or the members of the United Nations, if the</p>\n<p>General Assembly is not in session, immediately the Security Council ceases to</p>\n<p>deal with such matters.</p>\n<p>As alluded to in Article 10, Article 12 specifically prohibits the General Assembly</p>\n<p>to make any recommendation to the Security Council, if the Security Council is</p>\n<p>seized with any dispute or situation, unless the Security Council so requests.</p>\n<p>Article 13 mandates the General Assembly to initiate studies and to make</p>\n<h4 id=\"recommendations-for-purposes-of\">recommendations for purposes of:</h4>\n<p>(a) Promoting international cooperation in the political field and</p>\n<p>encouraging the progressive development of international law</p>\n<p>and its codification.</p>\n<p>(b) Promoting international cooperation in the economic, social,</p>\n<p>cultural, educational and health fields, and assisting in the</p>\n<p>realization of human rights and fundamental freedoms for all</p>\n<p>without distinction as to race, sex, language, or religion.</p>\n<p>The General Assembly is empowered by article 14 to make recommendations on</p>\n<p>measures for peaceful adjustment of any situation, while Article 15 provides for</p>\n<p>receipt and consideration by the General Assembly of annual or special reports</p>\n<p>from the Security Council. Article 16 provides for the General Assembly</p>\n<p>functions with respect to the International Trustee System.</p>\n<p>2.4.1 (c) Meetings of the General Assembly</p>\n<p>The General Assembly meets in regular and special sessions as required. Special</p>\n<p>sessions are convened by the Secretary General at the request of the Security</p>\n<p>Council, or of the majority of the member states. See Article 20.</p>\n<p>The General Assembly adopts its own rules of procedure. It also elects a</p>\n<p>president. It may establish organs it deems necessary in order to perform its</p>\n<p>functions.</p>\n<p>2.4.1 (d) Voting in the General Assembly Voting in the General</p>\n<p>Assembly is provided for under Article 18 paragraph 1, which states that each</p>\n<p>member of the General Assembly shall have one vote. Paragraph 2 requires that</p>\n<p>important questions be decided by a two-thirds majority of members present and</p>\n<p>voting. The important issues include maintenance of international peace and</p>\n<p>security, the election of non permanent members of the Security Council, the</p>\n<p>election of members of the Economic and Social Council, (ECOSOC), the</p>\n<p>members of the Trusteeship System, the admission of new members of the</p>\n<p>United Nations, the suspension of rights and privileges of membership, the</p>\n<p>expulsion of members, questions relating to the operations of the Trusteeship</p>\n<p>System, and budgetary questions.</p>\n<p>A member of the United Nations which is in arrears in the payment of financial</p>\n<p>contributions to the organization has no right to vote in the General Assembly,</p>\n<p>if the amount or its equivalent exceeds the amount of contribution due from such</p>\n<p>a member for the preceding two full years.</p>\n<p>The General Assembly has discretion to allow such a member to vote if the</p>\n<p>General Assembly is satisfied that the failure to pay is due to conditions beyond</p>\n<p>the control of the members17. See Article 19.</p>\n<p>2.5 (a) The Security Council</p>\n<p>This is provided for under Article V of the United Nations Charter. Article 23.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 1 provides for that the Council shall comprise fifteen members of the</p>\n<h4 id=\"united-nations-it-also-provides-for-the-five-permanent-members-which-include\">United Nations. It also provides for the five permanent members, which include:</h4>\n<p>The Republic of China, France, the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</p>\n<p>(Russia), the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, and</p>\n<p>United States of America. There is also provision for election by the General</p>\n<p>Assembly of ten other members. In this regard the General Assembly is guided</p>\n<p>by the contribution of members of the United Nations to the maintenance of</p>\n<p>international peace and security, other purposes of the organization and also</p>\n<p>equitable geographical distribution.</p>\n<p>The non- permanent members of the Security Council are elected for a period of</p>\n<p>2 years. According to paragraph 2, in the first election of the nonpermanent</p>\n<p>members, after the increase of membership of the Security Council from eleven</p>\n<p>to fifteen, two of the additional members shall be chosen for a term of one year.</p>\n<p>It also provides that a retiring member shall not be eligible for re election.</p>\n<p>Each member of the Security Council is entitled to have one representative. On</p>\n<p>voting and the rule of great power or great power unanimity, or veto powers of</p>\n<p>the Security Council, see Articles 28 – 32.</p>\n<p>17</p>\n<p>This provision was at one time applied to Mozambique during the civil war. I</p>\n<p>think it has also been applied to some other county in West Africa. There may</p>\n<p>be others.</p>\n<p>For current membership see the UN website, but until recently it stood as</p>\n<p>follows: France, Germany, Guinea, Mexico, Pakistan, Russia Federation, Spain,</p>\n<p>Syria, Arab Republic, United Kingdom, United States of America, Angola,</p>\n<p>Bulgaria, Cameroon, China and Chile.</p>\n<p>2.5 (b) The Functions and Powers of the Security Council</p>\n<p>These are spelled out in articles 24 – 26. In particular Article 24 paragraph 1 states</p>\n<p>that in order to ensure prompt and effective action by the United Nations, its</p>\n<p>members confer on the Security Council primary responsibility for the</p>\n<p>maintenance of international peace and security, and agree that in carrying out its</p>\n<p>duties under this responsibility the Security Council acts on their behalf.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 provides that in discharging these duties the Security Council shall</p>\n<p>act in accordance with the purposes and principles of the United States. The</p>\n<p>Specific powers granted to the Security Council for the discharge of these duties</p>\n<p>are laid down in Chapter VI, VII, VIII and XII. In short they include: pacific</p>\n<p>settlement of disputes, actions with respect to threats to peace, breaches of peace,</p>\n<p>acts of aggression, regional arrangements and international trusteeship system.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3 of Article 24 requires the Security Council to submit annual reports</p>\n<p>and whenever it considers it necessary special reports to the General Assembly</p>\n<p>for consideration.</p>\n<p>Article 25 enjoins members of the United Nations to agree to accept to carry out</p>\n<p>the decisions of the Security Council, while Article 26 confers on the Security</p>\n<p>Council responsibility for formulating plans to regulate armaments in</p>\n<p>collaboration with the Military Staff Committee, provided for under Article 47.</p>\n<p>This arrangement is intended to promote the establishment of international</p>\n<p>peace and security. Does it work?</p>\n<p>2.5 (c) The Challenges of Security Council</p>\n<p>The Security Council is a well-intentioned organ of the United Nations. However</p>\n<p>well intentioned it may be, it has had some limitations and challenges in the past</p>\n<p>and even in our present time.</p>\n<p>The main problem facing this organ is getting the necessary consensus within the</p>\n<p>Security Council on any given issue or case. The Human Rights Watch 2000</p>\n<p>came up with an interesting observation regarding the performance of the</p>\n<h4 id=\"security-council-the-report-says\">Security Council. The report says:</h4>\n<p>“As the Council functions today, with five permanent members free</p>\n<p>to                 exercise their vetoes for the most parochial reasons, it</p>\n<p>(Security Council)           can-not be counted on to authorize intervention</p>\n<p>even in direct circumstances.</p>\n<p>China and Russia seem pre- occupied by perceived analogies to</p>\n<p>Tibet and</p>\n<p>Chechnya. The United States is sometimes paralysed by an</p>\n<p>isolationist</p>\n<p>Congress and a risk averse Pentagon. Britain and France have let</p>\n<p>commercial            Or cultural ties to stand in the way”</p>\n<p>With the above one may conclude with the position the Security Council adopted</p>\n<p>when the United States and the coalition of the willing invaded Iraq and over</p>\n<p>threw the government.</p>\n<p>2.6 (a) The Economic and Social Council</p>\n<p>The Economic and Social Council is a principal organ of the United Nations as</p>\n<p>stipulated in Article 7 of the United Nations Charter.</p>\n<p>The composition of ECOSOC is provided for under Article 61 paragraph 1. It</p>\n<p>says: The Economic and Social Council shall consist of fifty-four members of</p>\n<p>the United Nations, elected by the General Assembly.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 states that: subject to the provisions of paragraph 3, eighteen</p>\n<p>members of the Economic and Social Council shall be elected each year for a</p>\n<p>term of three years. A retiring member shall be eligible for re-election.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3 provides for the election of the members of the expanded ECOSOC</p>\n<p>and their terms of office are staggered to allow for continuity. Each member of</p>\n<p>ECOSOC has one representative. The fifty-four members elected for a three year</p>\n<h4 id=\"period-are-fielded-on-the-basis-of-geographical-distribution-as-follows\">period are fielded on the basis of geographical distribution as follows:</h4>\n<p>14 Seats - African States</p>\n<p>11 Seats - Asian States</p>\n<p>06 Seats - Eastern European States</p>\n<p>10 Seats - Latin American and Caribbean States</p>\n<p>13 Seats – Western European States and Others</p>\n<p>The African States who are members of ECOSOC are: Benin, Burundi, Congo,</p>\n<p>Egypt, Ethiopia, Ghana, Kenya, Libya Arab Jamaharia, Mozambique, Nigeria,</p>\n<p>Senegal, South Africa, Uganda and Zimbabwe.</p>\n<p>2.6 (b) The Mandate of ECOSOC</p>\n<p>This Council plays a vital role in the United Nations System. It coordinates the</p>\n<h4 id=\"work-of-14-united-nations-specialized-agencies-these-are\">work of 14 United Nations specialized agencies. These are:</h4>\n<p>It also coordinates 10 functional regional commissions, receives reports from 11</p>\n<p>United Nations funds programmes, which include: the Economic Commission</p>\n<p>for Africa (ECA), Europe, Latin America and Caribbean, Economic and Social</p>\n<p>Commission for Asia, and Economic and Social Commission for Western Asia.</p>\n<p>ECOSOC is also responsible for providing policy recommendations to the</p>\n<p>United Nations System and member states.</p>\n<p>The United Nations Charter mandates ECOSOC to be responsible for</p>\n<p>promoting higher standards of living, full employment, economic and social</p>\n<p>progress, identifying solutions to international economic, social and health</p>\n<p>problems, facilitating international cultural educational cooperation, as well as</p>\n<p>encouraging respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.</p>\n<p>In executing its mandate ECOSOC works in consultation with academics,</p>\n<p>business sector representatives and registered non-governmental organizations</p>\n<p>which number more than 2100.</p>\n<p>2.6 (c) The Meetings of ECOSOC</p>\n<p>ECOSOC holds a four-week main session in July of every year and it alternates</p>\n<p>between New York and Geneva venues.</p>\n<p>The main or substantive session comprises high-powered representatives, which</p>\n<p>includes national cabinet ministers, heads of international agencies and high level</p>\n<p>officials. The main focus is on selected themes of global importance.</p>\n<p>Last years main session was chaired by ECOSOC President, H.E Gert</p>\n<p>Rosental. It considered issues pertaining to promoting an intergrated approach</p>\n<p>to rural development in developing countries for poverty eradication and</p>\n<p>sustainable development.</p>\n<p>ECOSOC is under the guidance of the bureau which is elected by the council at</p>\n<p>large at the beginning of each annual session. The main functions of the bureau</p>\n<p>is to propose (1) The agenda, (2) Draw up a programme of Work, (3) Organize</p>\n<p>the session with the support of the United Nations Secretariate.</p>\n<p>2.6 (d) Achievements of ECOSOC</p>\n<p>ECOSOC has in recent years played a leading role in important policy areas. For</p>\n<p>example in 1999 a high level segment issued a manifesto on poverty. This</p>\n<p>manifesto was instrumental in the formulation of the Millennium Development</p>\n<p>Goals approved by the United Nations Millennium summit in New York. It</p>\n<p>should also be remembered that the ministerial declaration of the high level</p>\n<p>segment in 2000 proposed specific actions to address digital divide leading</p>\n<p>directly to the formation of the Information and Communications</p>\n<p>Technology Task force.</p>\n<p>In the year 2002 ECOSOC’s consideration of African development culminated</p>\n<p>into the first ever formal international endorsement of the New Partnership for</p>\n<p>African Development (NEPAD).</p>\n<p>In 1998 ECOSOC during their non substantive sessions initiated a tradition of</p>\n<p>meeting each April with finance ministers heading key committees of Bretton</p>\n<p>Woods Institutions, the World Bank and International Monetary Fund. These</p>\n<p>consultations paved the way for the success of the international conference on</p>\n<p>financing for development held in March 2002 in Monterrey Mexico.</p>\n<p>2.7 The Trusteeship Council</p>\n<p>The Council is one of the main organs of the United Nations. The composition</p>\n<p>of the Trusteeship Council is provided for under article 86 of the United Nations</p>\n<h4 id=\"charter-paragraph-1-states-as-follows\">Charter. Paragraph 1 states as follows:</h4>\n<p>“The Trusteeship Council shall consist of the following</p>\n<p>members of the United Nations</p>\n<p>(a) Those members administering trustee territories</p>\n<p>(b) Such of those members mentioned by name in Article 23 as Are</p>\n<p>not administering trust territories: China, France, Russian</p>\n<p>Federation, U.K, and USA.</p>\n<p>(c) As many other members elected for 3 year terms by the General</p>\n<p>Assembly as may be necessary to ensure that the total number</p>\n<p>Of members of the Trusteeship Council is equally divided</p>\n<p>Between these members of the United Nations, which</p>\n<p>administer   Trust territories and those, which do not.</p>\n<p>The Trusteeship Council suspended its operations on 1st November 1994, after</p>\n<p>the independence of Palau, the last remaining United Nations territories on 1st</p>\n<p>October 1994. The Trusteeship Council had earlier on 25th May 1994, amended</p>\n<p>its rules of procedure and agreed to stop their annual meetings and instead meet</p>\n<p>as occasion required, or by its decision, or at the decision of its president, or at</p>\n<p>the request of the majority members of the General Assembly or Security</p>\n<p>Council. The reason for this is that the aims of the Trustee Council have been</p>\n<p>fulfilled, because all trust territories have got self government or independence,</p>\n<p>either as separate states or by joining neighbouring independent countries</p>\n<p>2.7 The International Court of Justice (ICJ)</p>\n<p>The International Court of Justice is another principal organ of the United</p>\n<h4 id=\"nations-article-92-of-the-united-nations-provides-as-follows\">Nations. Article 92 of the United Nations provides as follows:</h4>\n<p>“The International Court of Justice shall be the principal organ</p>\n<p>of the United Nations. It shall function in accordance with the</p>\n<p>annexed             Statute, which is based upon the Statute of the</p>\n<p>Permanent Court of</p>\n<p>Justice16 and forms an integral part of the present Charter.”</p>\n<p>The Statute of this court is also part of the United Nations Charter. According</p>\n<p>to Article 93 paragraph 1 of the United Nations Charter all members of the</p>\n<p>United Nations are by virtue of membership parties to the Statute of the</p>\n<p>International Court of Justice.</p>\n<p>Provision is also made for a state, which is not a member of the United Nations</p>\n<p>to become a party to the Statute of the International Court of Justice. The</p>\n<p>General Assembly, upon the recommendations of the Security Council,</p>\n<p>determines the conditions of becoming such a party – see Article 93 paragraph</p>\n<p>2.</p>\n<p>According to Article 94 each member of the United Nations undertakes to</p>\n<p>comply with the decisions of the International Court of Justice, in any case to</p>\n<p>which it is a party.</p>\n<p>In the event that any party to a case fails to perform the obligations incumbent</p>\n<p>upon it under the judgment handed down by the court, the other party is entitled</p>\n<p>to have recourse to the Security Council, which may if it sees it necessary, make</p>\n<p>recommendations or decide upon measures to be taken to give effect to the</p>\n<p>judgment of the ICJ. See Article 42 (2) of the ICJ.</p>\n<p>The existence of the Court does not preclude or prevent the members of the</p>\n<p>United Nations from accessing other tribunals with a view to getting a solution</p>\n<p>16</p>\n<p>The Permanent Court of International Justice was created by the League of Nations between 1922 – 1946.</p>\n<p>to their problems, if the member states have agreed to refer their problem to</p>\n<p>such other tribunals. See Article 95.</p>\n<p>In terms Article 96 paragraph 1, the General Assembly or Security Council may</p>\n<p>request the International Court of Justice to give an advisory opinion on any legal</p>\n<p>question. See the Reparations Case17 discussed in part I of this course.</p>\n<p>The other organs of the United Nations and the specialized agencies may at any</p>\n<p>time be authorized by the Secretary General to request the ICJ to give an advisory</p>\n<p>opinion on legal questions arising within the scope of their activities.</p>\n<p>The court therefore deals with civil matters. It is manned by 15 judges. The full</p>\n<h4 id=\"list-is-as-follows\">list is as follows:</h4>\n<p>1 Shi Jiuyong (China) (President)</p>\n<p>2 Raymond Ranjeva (Madagasca) Vice President</p>\n<p>3 Gilbert Guillaume (France) Judge</p>\n<p>4 Abdul G. Koroma (Sierra Leone) Judge</p>\n<p>5 Vladlen S. Vereshchetin (Russian Federation) (Judge)</p>\n<p>6 Rosalyn Higgins (U.K.) (Judge)</p>\n<p>7 Gonzalo Parra-Arranguren (Venezuela) (Judge)</p>\n<p>8 Pieter H. Kooijmans (Netherlands) (Judge)</p>\n<p>9 Francisco Rezek (Brazil) (Judge)</p>\n<p>10 Awn Shawkat Al- Khasawneh (Jordan) (Judge)</p>\n<p>11 Thomas Buergenthal (USA) Judge</p>\n<p>12 Nabil Elarby (Egypt) Judge</p>\n<p>13 Hisashi Owada (Japan) (Jugde)</p>\n<p>14 Bruno Simma (Germany) (Judge)</p>\n<p>15 Peter Tomka (Slovania) (Judge)</p>\n<p>The Registrar is Mr. Philippe Couvreur from Belgium18.</p>\n<p>17</p>\n<p>See: ICJ Reports (1949) p 174. This case is also documented in DJ Harris: Cases and Materials on</p>\n<p>International Law, Sweet and Maxwell, 1983, Third Edition p 114.</p>\n<p>18</p>\n<p>For further information about the court and its judgments see www.icj.org</p>\n<p>2.8 (a) The International Criminal Court Established by the Rome</p>\n<p>Statute</p>\n<p>It is important to draw a distinction between the International Court of Justice</p>\n<p>and the International Criminal Court. The former was established in 1945 after</p>\n<p>the San Francisco Conference, with civil jurisdiction on matters between states.</p>\n<p>The latter is a relatively recent creation, as we will see later.</p>\n<p>As the name suggests the latter is an international court with criminal jurisdiction.</p>\n<p>2.8 (b) History and Background</p>\n<p>The United Nations has been concerned about establishing an international</p>\n<p>criminal court since the 1950s, in order to bring justice those responsible for</p>\n<p>crimes such as genocide. This is reflected in the General Assembly resolution</p>\n<h4 id=\"260-of-december-1948-in-which-it-was-inter-alia-stated-that\">260 of December 1948, in which it was inter alia stated that:</h4>\n<p>“Recognising that at all periods of history genocide has inflicted</p>\n<p>great            loss on humanity; and being convinced that, in order to</p>\n<p>liberate mankind             from such an odious scourge, international</p>\n<p>cooperation is required”</p>\n<p>The said General Assembly resolution adopted the Convention on the</p>\n<p>Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide. Article I of the</p>\n<p>Convention identifies genocide as “a crime under international law”. Article VI</p>\n<p>provides that persons charged with genocide shall be tried by a competent</p>\n<p>tribunal of the state in the territory of which the act was committed or by such</p>\n<p>international penal tribunal as may have jurisdiction.</p>\n<p>The General Assembly also called upon the International Law</p>\n<p>Commission to study the desirability of establishing an international judicial</p>\n<p>organ for the trial of persons charged with genocide. International Law</p>\n<p>Commission came to a conclusion that the establishment of the international</p>\n<p>court to try persons charged with genocide or other crimes of similar gravity was</p>\n<p>desirable and possible.</p>\n<p>2.9 (c) Preliminary Efforts on Founding of the International Criminal</p>\n<p>Court</p>\n<p>Accordingly the General Assembly established a committee to prepare proposals</p>\n<p>for the founding of a criminal court. The Committee commenced work on the</p>\n<p>first draft Statute in 1951, which draft statute was revised in 1953. The General</p>\n<p>Assembly however decided to postpone deliberation of the draft Statute pending</p>\n<p>the adoption of a definition of: what constitutes aggression?</p>\n<p>From 1953 the question of establishing an international court has been</p>\n<p>considered periodically. In 1989 Trinidad and Tobago requested the General</p>\n<p>Assembly to request the International Law Commission to resume work on</p>\n<p>the International Criminal Court, with a wider jurisdiction to cover drug</p>\n<p>trafficking. The General Assembly did as requested and the International Law</p>\n<p>Commission went on with its work.</p>\n<p>In 1993, when the turbulences in the former Yugoslavia broke out, and ethnic</p>\n<p>cleansing took place, war and crimes against humanity were committed. These</p>\n<p>once again were brought to the international community’s attention.</p>\n<p>In order to bring an end to the wide spread suffering, the United Nations Security</p>\n<p>Council established an ad hock International Tribunal for the former</p>\n<p>Yugoslavia. This tribunal was intended to bring to justice the individuals who</p>\n<p>were responsible for the atrocities. It was expected that this would bring about</p>\n<p>deterrence.</p>\n<p>In the mean time the International Law Commission completed its work on</p>\n<p>the draft Statute for the international criminal court and submitted the same to</p>\n<p>the General Assembly.</p>\n<p>2.8 (d) Establishment of the International Criminal Court</p>\n<p>The General Assembly then established an ad hock Committee on the</p>\n<p>establishment of an International Criminal Court. Its main brief was to</p>\n<p>consider the major substantive issues arising from the draft Statute. The said ad</p>\n<p>hock Committee met two times in 1995. After the General Assembly considered</p>\n<p>the ad hock Committee’s report, it created the Preparatory Committee on the</p>\n<p>Establishment of an International Criminal Court, to prepare a widely</p>\n<p>acceptable and consolidated draft text for submission to a diplomatic conference.</p>\n<p>This Preparatory Committee, which met from 1996 to 1998, held its final session</p>\n<p>between March and April 1998 and completed its drafting work.</p>\n<p>At its 52nd session, the General Assembly decided to convene a Diplomatic</p>\n<p>Conference of Plenipotentiaries on the Establishment of an International</p>\n<p>Criminal Court. The said conference was held in Rome, Italy from 15 – 17 July</p>\n<p>1998 to finalise and adopt a convention on the establishment of an international</p>\n<p>criminal court.</p>\n<p>The Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court entered into force on 1st</p>\n<p>July 2002, in accordance with article 126. 139 state representatives have so far</p>\n<p>signed the statute. 21 Uganda signed the Statute on 17 March 1999 and ratified it</p>\n<p>in June 2002</p>\n<p>21</p>\n<h4 id=\"ratification-and-implementation-gt-ratification-of-the-rome-statute\">Ratification and Implementation &gt; Ratification of the Rome Statute:</h4>\n<p>Supporting ratification of the Rome Statute of the ICC is crucial to making the</p>\n<p>membership in the ICC truly global and universal. In order for the ICC to</p>\n<p>succeed, a growing majority of the world’s nations must support the Court and</p>\n<p>the Rome Statute and actively cooperate in areas such as providing evidence,</p>\n<p>surrendering indicted individuals and holding national trials. The Rome Statute</p>\n<p>entered into force after achieving 60 ratifications in July 2002, decades earlier</p>\n<p>than predicted. Now, only a few years later, the number of ratifications has risen</p>\n<p>from 66 to 105. The 105th ratification by Japan in July 2007 represented an</p>\n<p>important milestone in advancing towards universal ratification of the Rome</p>\n<p>Statute.</p>\n<p>For most nations, the key challenge in convincing governments to consider</p>\n<p>ratification is educational. The CICC plays an important role in terms of</p>\n<p>providing accurate and updated information about international justice and the</p>\n<p>ICC. The CICC also assists countries in addressing any possible constitutional</p>\n<p>and legislative obstacles to ratification, in addition to actively engaging in efforts</p>\n<p>to stimulate and generate the necessary political will for countries to become</p>\n<p>State Parties to the Court.</p>\n<p>The Coalition has redoubled its ratification efforts in regions of the world, such</p>\n<p>as Asia and the Middle East, which are underrepresented at the ICC. Political</p>\n<p>changes in recent years have indicated a new openness in some previously ‘ICC-</p>\n<p>hesitant’ countries. Out of 139 signatories to the Rome Statute, 100 have</p>\n<p>subsequently ratified the Rome Statute, while an additional 39 countries have not</p>\n<p>yet ratified. The CICC’s</p>\n<p>2.8 (e) The Jurisdiction of the International Criminal Court</p>\n<p>The International Criminal Court’s jurisdiction extends to crimes that were</p>\n<p>committed after 1 July 2002, when the Rome Statute entered into force.22</p>\n<p>The international Criminal Court’s jurisdiction is provided for under Article 5 (1)</p>\n<h4 id=\"of-the-rome-statute-it-states-as-follows\">of the Rome Statute. It states as follows:</h4>\n<p>“The jurisdiction of the court shall be limited to the most serious</p>\n<p>crimes of            concern to the international community as a whole. The</p>\n<p>court has jurisd-          iction in accordance with this statute with respect</p>\n<h4 id=\"to-the-following-crimes\">to the following crimes:</h4>\n<p>(a) The crime of genocide,</p>\n<p>(b) Crimes against humanity,</p>\n<p>(c) War crimes,</p>\n<p>(d) The crime of aggression.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 provides that the court shall exercise jurisdiction over the crime of</p>\n<p>aggression once a provision is adopted in accordance with articles 121 and 123</p>\n<p>defining the crime and setting out the conditions under which the court shall</p>\n<p>exercise jurisdiction with respect to this crime. Such a provision shall be</p>\n<p>consistent with the relevant provisions of the Charter of the United Nations.</p>\n<p>For specific crimes see articles 16, 17, 18 and 20 (a) – (d).</p>\n<p>2.8 (f) Applicable Principles of Law</p>\n<p>The general principles of criminal law, which are applicable to the court are</p>\n<p>provided for under part 3, that is from articles 22 – 25. These include the</p>\n<h4 id=\"following\">following:</h4>\n<p>ratification campaign strongly encourages these signatory states to continue</p>\n<p>their commitment to international justice and join the Court. Furthermore,</p>\n<p>several non-signatory states have declared their interest in ratifying the</p>\n<p>Rome          Statute.    http://www.iccnow.org/?mod=romeratification</p>\n<p>2/9/2009</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>See article by Rammy Park, United Nations Chronicles.</p>\n<p>A person shall not be criminally responsible under the statute, unless the conduct</p>\n<p>in question constituted a crime, at the time the act takes place, a crime within the</p>\n<p>jurisdiction of the court;</p>\n<p>A person convicted by the court should only be punished in accordance with the</p>\n<p>statute;</p>\n<p>No person shall be criminally responsible for under the statute for conduct, prior</p>\n<p>to the entry into force of the statute, that is 1st July 2002.</p>\n<p>2.8 (g) The Organs of the Court</p>\n<p>The organs of the court are provided for under article 35, which states as follows:</p>\n<p>(a) The Presidency</p>\n<p>(b) An Appeals Division, which comprises the Trial Division and a Pre</p>\n<p>Trial Division,</p>\n<p>(c) Office of the Prosecutor; and</p>\n<p>(d) The Registry.</p>\n<p>2.8 (h) The Judges of the Court</p>\n<p>The court comprises 18 judges of whom 7 are female. Africa has 3</p>\n<p>representatives, all of whom are women. These judges were inaugurated at the</p>\n<p>Dutch Parliament on 12 March 2003.23 For a full list refer to the website of the</p>\n<p>court.24</p>\n<p>23</p>\n<p>See Rammy Park supra.</p>\n<p>24</p>\n<p>LIST A judges appointed for 9 years: CLARK, Maureen Harding, from</p>\n<p>Ireland, Female; DIARRA,</p>\n<p>Fatoumata Demble, from Mali; FULFORD, Adrian, from UK, Male; HUDSON-</p>\n<p>PHILIPS, Karl T from</p>\n<p>Trinidad and Tobago, Male; JORDA, Claude from France, Male, ODIO</p>\n<p>BENITO, Elizabeth from Costa</p>\n<p>Rica, female; PIKIS, Gheorghios M, from Cyprus, male; SLADE, Tuiloma Neroi,</p>\n<p>from Samoa, male; SONG, Sang-hyun, from the Republic of Korea, male;</p>\n<p>STEINER, Sylivia H.de Figueiredo, from Bazil, female.</p>\n<p>LIST B:BLATTMANN, Rene from Bolvia, Male (6) years; KAUL, Hans-Peter</p>\n<p>from Germany, male, 3 years; KIRSCH, Phillipe, Canada, male (6) years;</p>\n<p>KOURULA, Erkki from Finiland, male, (3) years; KUENYEHIA, Akua from</p>\n<p>Ghana, felale (3 ) years; PILLAY, Navanetham, from South Africa, female, (6)</p>\n<p>years, POLITI, Mauro from Italy, male, (6) years; USACKA, Anita, from Lativia,</p>\n<h4 id=\"female-3-years-for-further-rading-on-the-court-see-christopher-keith-hall\">female, (3) years. For further rading on the Court see: Christopher Keith Hall:</h4>\n<p>The Role of the International Criminal Court in</p>\n<p>The establishment of an international Criminal Court has been characterized as</p>\n<p>the missing link in the international legal system. The court will deal with</p>\n<p>individuals. This in a way is viewed as an enforcement mechanism, to punish</p>\n<p>those responsible for acts of genocide and gross violations of human rights,</p>\n<p>which have hitherto been unpunished. War crimes and crimes against humanity</p>\n<p>have been perpetrated in many places of the world, for example, Cambodia in</p>\n<p>the 1970’s where an estimated 2 million people were killed by Khmer Rouge. In</p>\n<p>armed conflicts in Mozambique, Liberia, El Salvador and other countries, lives</p>\n<p>have been lost. Massacre of civilians have taken place in Algeria and the Great</p>\n<p>Lakes region of Africa.</p>\n<p>2.8 (i) Developments Involving Ugandans</p>\n<p>On or about 29th January 2004, the International Criminal Court Prosecutor Luis</p>\n<p>Moreno Ocampo announced that there was sufficient evidence to start inquiry</p>\n<p>into the grave human rights violations committed by the lorld’s Resistance</p>\n<p>Army. This would target Joseph Kony himself and his commanders. The</p>\n<p>problem though is that some of those commanders are not under Kony’s</p>\n<p>command voluntarily.25 There is also a pertinent question as to whether or not</p>\n<p>the Government of Uganda also would consider taking the Sudanese</p>\n<p>Government to the International Criminal Court for having armed, trained and</p>\n<p>sheltered the lord’s resistance army.26 It would be interesting to see who among</p>\n<p>our people in Uganda and even Africa will be brought prosecuted before the</p>\n<p>International Criminal Court. I think your guess is as good as mine on this issue.</p>\n<p>2.9 The Secretariat.</p>\n<p>Prosecuting Genocide, other crimes Against Humanity and Serious Violations of</p>\n<p>Humanitarian Law, in</p>\n<p>Gudmundur Alfredsson, Jonas Grimheden, Betram G.Ramcharan and Alfred de</p>\n<p>Zayas: International</p>\n<p>Human Rights Monitoring Mechanism, Essays in honour of Jakob Th. Moller,</p>\n<p>The Raul Wallenberg Institute Human Rights Library, Volume 7, Martinus</p>\n<p>Nijhoff Publishes, The Hague/Boston/London, pp457</p>\n<p>– 467.</p>\n<p>25</p>\n<p>See Sunday Vision, February 1, 2004, p 2 column 3.</p>\n<p>26</p>\n<p>See also views of Carlos Rodriguez, in The New Vision, Wednesday February</p>\n<p>4, 2004, p.20 and 21 column 2.</p>\n<p>Article 97 of the Charter of the United Nations provides that the Secretariat shall</p>\n<p>comprise a Secretary General and such staff at the organization may require. The</p>\n<p>Secretary General shall be appointed by the General Assembly upon</p>\n<p>recommendation of the Security Council. The Secretary General is the Chief</p>\n<p>Administrative Officer of the United Nations.</p>\n<p>According to Article 98 the Secretary General shall act in that capacity in all</p>\n<p>meetings of the General Assembly, of the Security Council, of the Economic and</p>\n<p>Social Council, and of the Trusteeship Council and shall perform such other</p>\n<p>functions as are entrusted to him by these organs. The Secretary General shall</p>\n<p>make an annual report to the General Assembly on the work of the organization.</p>\n<p>The Secretary General is mandated to bring to the attention of the Security</p>\n<p>Council, any matter, which in his opinion may threaten the maintenance of</p>\n<p>international peace and security. (see article 99).</p>\n<p>The Secretary General is independent and does not receive instructions from any</p>\n<p>government or from any external authority to the organization. In executing the</p>\n<p>duties of his office, the Secretary General together with his staff are required to</p>\n<p>reflect their independence. (see article 100)</p>\n<p>Article 101 paragraphs 1 – 2 provide for the appointment of the Secretariat and</p>\n<p>their assignment to the ECOSOC, and Trusteeship Council and other organs of</p>\n<p>the United Nations.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3 provides that paramount consideration in the employment of the</p>\n<p>staff and in determination of conditions of service, shall be the necessity of</p>\n<p>securing the highest standards of efficiency, competence, and integrity. Due</p>\n<p>regard shall be paid to the importance of recruiting staff on as a wide</p>\n<p>geographical basis as possible. (Are the people employed by the United Nations</p>\n<p>system the best or are there other considerations – eg old boy old girl net work,</p>\n<p>consideration of issues such as family or close ties with those who worked in</p>\n<p>diplomatic missions abroad?</p>\n<p>The duties executed by the Secretariat are varied and range from administering</p>\n<p>peacekeeping operations to mediating international disputes, from surveying</p>\n<p>economic and social trends and problems, to preparing studies on human rights</p>\n<p>and sustainable development. The Secretariat also provides information to the</p>\n<p>world’s communications media about the activities of the United Nations. It also</p>\n<p>organizes international conferences on issues of global concern and interprets</p>\n<p>speeches as well as translating documents into official languages of the United</p>\n<p>Nations</p>\n<p>The Secretariat has a staff complement of 8,900 personnel, who are recruited</p>\n<p>from 170 countries.</p>\n<p>2.9.1 The United Nations’ Role in Human Rights Standard Setting</p>\n<p>As noted above one of the purposes of the United Nations is to promote respect</p>\n<p>for equal rights and respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms for all</p>\n<p>people. In order to do this the United Nations has deliberately developed a</p>\n<p>system of standard setting in the area of human rights. The very first effort by</p>\n<p>the United Nations in standard setting culminated into the Universal Declaration</p>\n<p>of Human Rights in 1948. We will later look at the UDHR in more details later.</p>\n<p>In pursuance of standard setting the United Nations works through studies, sub-</p>\n<p>commissions and working groups. For example following the subcommission’s</p>\n<p>study, which was undertaken by Francesco Caportorti, on the Rights of</p>\n<p>Persons Belonging to Minorities, a working group was established by the</p>\n<p>United Nations Commission on Human Rights, after Yugoslavia’s efforts to</p>\n<p>bring this issue to the world’s attention in 1978. The working group worked for</p>\n<p>more than 10 years and its work culminated into a Declaration on the Rights</p>\n<p>of Persons Belonging to National or Ethnic, Religious or Linguistic</p>\n<p>Minorities. The Declaration was adopted by the</p>\n<p>United Nations General Assembly in 1992.19</p>\n<p>The United Nations has been instrumental in formulating guidelines or codes of</p>\n<p>conduct, which prescribe the minimum required standards in a variety of</p>\n<p>situations so as to guarantee the enjoyment of human rights. There are many</p>\n<p>examples of these, but a few will suffice: The Standard Minimum Rules for</p>\n<p>the Treatment of Prisoners;20 adopted by the First United Nations Congress</p>\n<p>on the Prevention of Crime and Treatment of Offenders, held at Geneva in 1955</p>\n<p>and approved by the Economic and Social Council by its resolution 663 C</p>\n<p>(XXXIV) of 31 July 1957 and of 13 May 1977. The document was approved by</p>\n<p>the General Assembly by way of resolution on 1 December 1985.</p>\n<p>Another set of international standards is The Beijing Rules – The Treatment</p>\n<p>of Juveniles. The Codes of Conduct in various sectors are also facilitated by the</p>\n<p>United Nations. For example The Code of Conduct for Law Enforcement</p>\n<p>Officials, and also the United Nations Basic Principles on the Use of Force</p>\n<p>19</p>\n<p>See General Assembly Resolution 47/135 adopted on 18 December 1992.</p>\n<p>20</p>\n<p>These and other standards may be accessed in United Nations: Human Rights, A compilation of</p>\n<p>International Instruments, Volume 1 (First Part) United Nations, New York and Geneva, 1994.</p>\n<p>and Fire Arms, and The Basic Principles on the Independence of the</p>\n<p>Judiciary.</p>\n<p>What should be noted about these United Nations Standards is that they are not</p>\n<p>necessarily meant to be compulsory framework, but rather a moral high road</p>\n<p>reached by consensus towards good theory and practice in the relevant area.21</p>\n<h4 id=\"2-9-2-the-universal-declaration-of-human-rights-udhr-1948\">2.9.2 The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) 1948:</h4>\n<p>The first set of human rights and fundamental freedoms by the UN was the</p>\n<p>UDHR, a declaration of the UN General Assembly, adopted in Paris, France on</p>\n<p>10th December 1948. At the point in time the UN had only 56 members. Of these</p>\n<p>48 voted in favour of the declaration, without any member voting against it. Only</p>\n<p>8 members abstained. These are Byelorussia, Czechoslovakia, Poland, Saudi</p>\n<p>Arabia, South Africa, Ukraine, the USSR and Yugoslavia.</p>\n<p>According to Bailey30, the UDHR is regarded as the single most important</p>\n<p>document created in the 20th Century and has come to be regarded as the world’s</p>\n<p>standard for human rights. The UDHR draws life preserving messages from the</p>\n<p>past and is viewed as a foundation for building a world in which all human beings</p>\n<p>in the future, look forward to living in dignity and peace.</p>\n<p>The UDHR was proclaimed after the Second World War, when humanity</p>\n<p>witnessed the cruelty and inhumanity inflicted on the Jews in Europe, and the</p>\n<p>prisoners of war, in detention in Europe and Asia.</p>\n<p>The initial steps towards drafting an international bill of rights were taken in April</p>\n<p>1946, when Mrs. Eleanor Roosevelt, widow of President Franklin Roosevelt of</p>\n<p>the USA, was appointed to chair an interim group of 9 members. Later, that</p>\n<p>interim group culminated into a new Commission of Human Rights. The said</p>\n<p>Commission had 18 members, and was also chaired by Mrs. Eleanor Roosevelt.</p>\n<p>The Commission met for the first time in January 1947.</p>\n<p>21</p>\n<p>See J.J. Noeth SC ON ….., A Paper presented during………., Lesotho Sun Hotel, Maseru, …. 30 See</p>\n<p>Peter Bailey, OBE, AM : The Creation of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights,</p>\n<p>www.universalrights.net/main/creation.htm See also Kim Gleeson: World wide influence of the</p>\n<p>Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the international Bill of Rights on the same web page.</p>\n<p>Due to the polarization caused by ideological difference and the political</p>\n<p>orientation of the time, the Commission decided to develop a declaration rather</p>\n<p>than a treaty, which is binding in international law. A declaration on the other</p>\n<p>hand is a statement of importance and has high moral and political significance,</p>\n<p>and is more than a recommendation.</p>\n<p>The Commission decided that the declaration should contain both the civil and</p>\n<p>political rights and the economic and social rights. The Commission formulated</p>\n<p>the declaration and called it the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. This</p>\n<p>meant that it was to be a standard for all people, everywhere – whether they are</p>\n<p>male or female, black or white, capitalist or communist, victor or vanquished,</p>\n<p>rich or poor, for members of a majority or a minority in society. This is reflected</p>\n<p>in the preamble.</p>\n<p>The core substantive provisions of the declaration are found in 3 articles, namely:</p>\n<p>• Article 3, which guarantees every human being the right to life, liberty,</p>\n<p>and security of persons. The fore going are civil and political rights.</p>\n<p>• Article 22, which provides for the right to social security; and</p>\n<p>• Article 27, which provides for the right to freely participate in the cultural</p>\n<p>life, to enjoy arts and share in scientific advancement and its benefits, and</p>\n<p>the right to protection of the moral and material interests and freedoms</p>\n<p>set forth in the declaration.</p>\n<p>The rights in Article 22 and 27 may be characterized as economic, social and</p>\n<p>cultural rights.</p>\n<h4 id=\"2-9-3-the-significance-of-the-universal-declaration-of-human-rights\">2.9.3 The Significance of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights:</h4>\n<p>The UDHR has come to be accepted as an influential statement of standards</p>\n<p>world-wide. Various constitutions of the world, including Uganda’s have been</p>\n<p>inspired by the same, and many others constitutions make reference to it at least</p>\n<p>in the preamble. Courts too, in diverse nations refer to the provisions of the</p>\n<p>Universal Declaration of Human Rights. The UDHR has therefore become part</p>\n<p>of the fabric of the UN.</p>\n<p>The UDHR is referred to in the UN General Assembly resolutions and debates,</p>\n<p>eg in relation to the Declaration on the Granting of Independence and Colonial</p>\n<p>Countries and People’s in 1960.</p>\n<p>The UDHR was also referred to at the Human Rights Conference in Teheran,</p>\n<p>Iran in 1978, by representatives of 84 nations, as a common understanding of</p>\n<p>the inalienable rights of all peoples and constitutes an obligation for the members</p>\n<p>of the international community. The Vienna Declaration and Programme of</p>\n<p>Action 1993 also makes reference to the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.</p>\n<p>Most, if not all the provisions of the UDHR have almost certainly become part</p>\n<p>of international customary law. There are many countries of the world, which</p>\n<p>have ratified the ICCPR and ICESCR. The rights recognized in these twin</p>\n<p>covenants are stated and provided for in the Universal Declaration of Human</p>\n<p>Rights, which though not a legally binding treaty, ratified by states, it has become</p>\n<p>part of international customary law.</p>\n<p>In conclusion it is submitted that the promotion and protection of human rights</p>\n<p>today would not be what it is without this important instrument, the Universal</p>\n<p>Declaration of Human Rights.</p>\n<p>2.9 The United Nations Human Rights System</p>\n<p>The United Nations Human Rights system comprises a two track approach,</p>\n<p>namely the Charter based bodies and the Treaty based bodies.</p>\n<p>The Charter bases organs derive their mandate from the United Nations</p>\n<p>Charter. Examples of the Charter based bodies include: the General</p>\n<p>Assembly, the Economic and Social Council, and the Commission on Human</p>\n<p>Rights. Also included in this category are those bodies, which have been</p>\n<p>authorised by the above mentioned bodies, such as the Sub-commission on the</p>\n<p>Promotion and Protection of Human Rights, and the Commission on the status</p>\n<p>of Women.</p>\n<p>The Treaty based bodies include the Human Rights Committee, formed under</p>\n<p>the ICCPR. We will see more about this body later.</p>\n<h4 id=\"2-11-the-commission-on-human-rights\">2.11 The Commission on Human Rights:</h4>\n<p>The initial mandate of the Human Rights Commission was to submit proposals</p>\n<h4 id=\"and-recommendations-to-the-ecosoc-on-the-following\">and recommendations to the ECOSOC on the following:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-an-international-bill-of-human-rights\">1. An international bill of human rights.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-international-declarations-or-conventions-on-civil-liberties-the-status-of\">2. International declarations or conventions on civil liberties, the status of</h3>\n<p>women, freedom of information and similar matters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-protection-of-minorities\">3. The protection of minorities.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-prevention-of-discrimination-on-grounds-of-race-sex-language-or\">4. The prevention of discrimination on grounds of race, sex, language or</h3>\n<p>religion.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-any-matter-concerning-human-rights\">5. Any matter concerning human rights.</h3>\n<p>A sixth function was added in 1979 that is to assist ECOSOC in the coordination</p>\n<p>of activities concerning human rights in the United Nations system22.</p>\n<p>Since its first meeting in 1947, the Commission has led international initiatives to</p>\n<p>define, promote and protect human rights and fundamental freedoms. Its main</p>\n<p>achievements include the initial drafting of the Universal Declaration of Human</p>\n<p>Rights, the International Covenant on Economic Social and Cultural Rights</p>\n<p>(ICESCR) and the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR)</p>\n<p>1966, and the Optional Protocols32. These instruments are sometimes referred</p>\n<p>to as the International Bill of Rights.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Commission had a Sub-commission on Prevention of</p>\n<p>Discrimination and Protection of Minorities until 1999. The Subcommission</p>\n<p>was changed by ECOSOC to the Sub-commission on Promotion and</p>\n<p>22</p>\n<p>See ECOSOC Resolution 1979/36, which was approved by General Assembly Resolution 34/25. 32</p>\n<h4 id=\"for-details-see-bhagwati-murkerjee-in-gudmundur-alfredsson-et-al\">For details see: Bhagwati Murkerjee ………………………. In Gudmundur Alfredsson et al:</h4>\n<p>International     Human Rights Monitoring Mechanisms – Essays in Honour of Jakob Th. Moller, Martinus</p>\n<p>Nijhoff Publishers 2001, p 217.</p>\n<p>Protection of Human Rights. This Sub-commission is serviced by 26 experts,</p>\n<p>who act in their individual capacity.</p>\n<p>The United Nations Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC), established the</p>\n<p>Human Rights Commission, on 16th February 1946, in terms of Article 68 of the</p>\n<p>United Nations Charter23. The above-mentioned Article authorizes ECOSOC to</p>\n<p>set up Commission in the Economic and Social fields, and for the protection of</p>\n<p>human rights</p>\n<p>2.11 The Office of The High Commissioner On Human Rights</p>\n<p>Although the United Nations Commission on Human Rights was set up way</p>\n<p>back in 1946, the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner on Human</p>\n<p>Rights was created by the General Assembly Resolution 48/141 of December</p>\n<h3 id=\"1993-the-office-of-the-high-commissioner-is-located-in-geneva-with-a-liaison\">1993. The office of the High Commissioner is located in Geneva, with a liaison</h3>\n<p>office in New York</p>\n<p>The first High commissioner was Mr Ayala Laso, followed by the late Sergio</p>\n<p>Vierra de Mello, a Brazilian national, who took over from Mrs Mary Robinson,</p>\n<p>a former Irish President, on 12th September 2002. Mr Segio Vierra de Mello was</p>\n<p>killed in Iraq in….(August 2003???) The incumbent is Ms Bertie Ramcharan???</p>\n<p>According to the above mentioned resolution, the High Commissioner must be</p>\n<p>a person of high moral standing and personal integrity, and shall possess</p>\n<p>expertise including in the field of human rights, and the general knowledge and</p>\n<p>understanding of diverse cultures necessary for impartial, objective, non selective</p>\n<p>and effective performance of the duties of the High Commissioner.</p>\n<p>He/she is nominated by the Secretary General of the United Nations, and</p>\n<p>appointed by the General Assembly, with due regard to geographical rotation,</p>\n<p>and a fixed term of 4 years. He/she is at the rank of Under Secretary General of</p>\n<p>the United Nations.</p>\n<p>23</p>\n<p>See ECOSOC Resolution 5 (1).</p>\n<p>The High commissioner functions within the framework of the United Nations</p>\n<p>Charter, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, other international human</p>\n<p>rights instruments and international law including the obligations within this</p>\n<p>framework, to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity and domestic</p>\n<p>jurisdiction of states and to promote the universal respect for and observance of</p>\n<p>all human rights.</p>\n<p>She/he must be guided by the recognition that all human rights- civil – cultural</p>\n<p>– economic – political and social- are universal, indivisible, interdependent and</p>\n<p>interrelated and that, while the significance of national and regional particularities</p>\n<p>and various historical, cultural and religious backgrounds must be borne in mind,</p>\n<p>it is the duty of states to, regardless of their political, economic and cultural</p>\n<p>systems, to promote and protect human rights and fundamental freedoms.</p>\n<p>The High Commissioner is the United Nations official with principal</p>\n<p>responsibility for United Nations human rights activities under the direction and</p>\n<p>authority of the Secretary General. She/he operates within the framework of the</p>\n<p>over all competence, authority and decisions of the General Assembly, the</p>\n<p>Economic Social and Cultural Council, and the Commission on Human Rights.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-specific-functions-of-the-high-commissioner-are-as-follows\">The specific functions of the High Commissioner are as follows:</h4>\n<p>(a) To promote and protect the effective enjoyment by all of all civil,</p>\n<p>cultural,</p>\n<p>economic, political and social rights</p>\n<p>(b) To carry out the tasks assigned him/her by the competent bodies</p>\n<p>of the United Nations system in the field of human rights and to</p>\n<p>make recommendations to them with a view to improving the</p>\n<p>promotion and protection of human rights;</p>\n<p>(c) To promote and protect the realization of the right to development</p>\n<p>and to enhance support from the relevant bodies in the field of the</p>\n<p>United Nations system for this purpose;</p>\n<p>(d) To provide through the Centre for Human Rights, of the</p>\n<p>Secretariat and other appropriate institutions, advisory services and</p>\n<p>technical and financial assistance, at the request of the state concerned</p>\n<p>and, where appropriate, the regional human rights organizations, with</p>\n<p>a view to supporting actions and programmes in the field of human</p>\n<p>rights;</p>\n<p>(e) To coordinate the relevant United Nations education and public</p>\n<p>information programmes in the field of human rights;</p>\n<p>(f) To play an active role in removing the current obstacles and in meeting</p>\n<p>the challenges of the full realization of all human rights and in</p>\n<p>preventing the continuation of human rights violations through out</p>\n<p>the world, as reflected in the Vienna Declaration and Programme of</p>\n<p>Action;</p>\n<p>(g) To engage in a dialogue with all governments in the implementation</p>\n<p>of his/her mandate with a view to securing respect for all human</p>\n<p>rights;</p>\n<p>(h) To enhance international cooperation for the promotion and</p>\n<p>protection of all human rights;</p>\n<p>(i) To coordinate the human rights promotion and protection activities</p>\n<p>throughout the United Nations system;</p>\n<p>(j) To rationalize, adopt, strengthen and streamline the United Nations</p>\n<p>machinery in the field of human rights, with a view to improving its</p>\n<p>efficiency and effectiveness;</p>\n<p>(k) To carry out over all supervision of the Centre for Human Rights</p>\n<p>(now Office of High Commissioner for Human Rights)</p>\n<p>The High Commissioner reports annually on his/her activities, in accordance</p>\n<p>with his/her mandate to the Commission on Human Rights, through ECOSOC</p>\n<p>to the General Assembly.</p>\n<p>2.12 The Human Rights Committee</p>\n<p>In terms of Article 28 of the ICCPR, the states parties established the Human</p>\n<p>Rights Committee on 20th September 1976 and elected 18 independent experts</p>\n<p>as members, whose term of office commenced on 1st January 1977. Its first</p>\n<p>session was held in New York at the United Nations Headquarters in the Spring</p>\n<p>of 1977. Since then it has held three weeks plenary sessions per year, each</p>\n<p>preceded by a one week working group session. The Summer session takes place</p>\n<p>at the United Nations Geneva office.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-main-responsibilities-of-the-human-rights-committee-are\">The main responsibilities of the Human Rights Committee are:</h4>\n<p>The consideration of reports submitted by states parties under article 40 of the</p>\n<p>Covenant.</p>\n<p>The formulation of general comments under article 40 (4) of the ICCPR, that</p>\n<p>is clarifying or explaining the contents of the articles of the Covenant in</p>\n<p>order to assist the states parties to fulfill their reporting obligations.</p>\n<p>Examining communications from individuals alleging violations of any rights</p>\n<p>set forth in the Covenant as provided for in the Optional Protocol thereto.</p>\n<p>Some authors are of the view that the future of the Human Rights Committee</p>\n<p>should not lie in examination of state party reports, but rather in individual</p>\n<p>communication.24</p>\n<p>In terms of Article 28 of the ICCPR, the states parties established the Human</p>\n<p>Rights Committee on 20th September 1976 and elected 18 independent experts</p>\n<p>as members, whose term of office commenced on 1st January 1977. Its first</p>\n<p>session was held in New York, at the UN Headquarters, in the spring of 1977.</p>\n<p>Since then it has held 3 (three) week plenary sessions per year, each preceded by</p>\n<p>a one week working group session. The summer session takes place at the UN</p>\n<p>Geneva office.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee forms part of the complex of universal bodies</p>\n<p>both under the UN Charter and treaty organs. The Human Rights Committee’s</p>\n<p>work in a way is related to the work of the United Nations Human Rights</p>\n<p>Commission.</p>\n<h4 id=\"2-13-the-human-rights-commission-and-human-rights-committee\">2.13 The Human Rights Commission and Human Rights Committee:</h4>\n<p>24</p>\n<p>See Alfred de Zaya…..</p>\n<p>A Comparison and Contrast</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee and Human Rights Commission are very</p>\n<p>important bodies as far as human rights promotion and protection is concerned.</p>\n<p>As noted above, their work is interrelated. However, it is important to note that</p>\n<p>the Human Rights Commission, which is Charter based, is composed of 53</p>\n<p>states. The Commission meets each year in regular sessions, in March and April</p>\n<p>for six weeks in Geneva. The Human Rights Committee, which is Treaty based,</p>\n<p>alternates between New York (March – April) and Geneva June – July.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Commission was instituted on the 16th February 1946, while</p>\n<p>the Human Rights Committee came into existence on the 20th September 197,</p>\n<p>under the auspices of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights</p>\n<p>1966.</p>\n<p>Over 3000 delegates from member and observer states, and from</p>\n<p>nongovernmental organizations participate in the Human Rights Commission,</p>\n<p>while there are fewer representatives of human rights Non Governmental</p>\n<p>organisations, which have observer status in the proceedings of the Human</p>\n<p>Rights Committee..</p>\n<p>The members of the Human Rights Commission are representatives of their</p>\n<p>governments.25 The Human Rights Committee comprises 18 individual experts,</p>\n<p>who are knowledgeable on human rights issues. They do not represent their</p>\n<p>countries.</p>\n<p>The Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC) Resolution 123526 and 150327</p>\n<p>(XL VIII) apply to the Human Rights Commission and not the Committee.</p>\n<p>25</p>\n<p>It should be remembered that the members of the Sub Commission on the promotion and Protection of</p>\n<p>Human Rights. It was until 1999 known as the Sub Commission on the Prevention of Discrimination and</p>\n<p>Protection of Minorities. It is composed of 26 experts, who act in their individual capacity.</p>\n<p>26</p>\n<p>This resolution empowers the Commission to consider any source containing information about gross</p>\n<p>violation of human rights and to initiate a thorough study of the situation if that information reveals a</p>\n<p>consistent pattern of violation of human rights.</p>\n<p>27</p>\n<p>Resolution 1503 provides for dealing with communications relating to violations of human rights and</p>\n<p>fundamental freedoms.</p>\n<p>Resolution 1235 empowers the Human Rights Commission to consider any</p>\n<p>source containing information about gross violations of human rights and to</p>\n<p>initiate a thorough study of the situation if that information reveals a consistent</p>\n<p>pattern of violation of human rights. Resolution 1503 (XLVIII) provides for the</p>\n<p>procedure for dealing with communications relating to violations of human</p>\n<p>rights and fundamental freedoms.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Commission does not handle state reports on</p>\n<p>implementation of human rights treaties. This is undertaken by the Human</p>\n<p>Rights Committee as provided for under article 40 (1) of the ICCPR. These state</p>\n<p>reports, together with individual communication provided for under the</p>\n<p>Optional Protocol, are handled by the Human Rights Committee.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Commission’s procedures and mechanisms are mandated to</p>\n<p>examine, monitor and publicly report either on human rights situations in</p>\n<p>specific countries or territories through what is known as country mechanisms</p>\n<p>or mandates, or on major phenomena of human rights violations world wide via</p>\n<p>what is called thematic mechanisms or mandates. These procedures are</p>\n<p>sometimes referred to as the special procedures of the Commission on Human</p>\n<p>Rights.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee’s mandate is narrower compared to the</p>\n<p>Commission’s mandate</p>\n<p>In the recent past the Commission under ECOSOC resolution 1990/48 of 25th</p>\n<p>May 1990, was mandated to deal with acute human rights situations in the most</p>\n<p>expeditious way. As a result the following special sessions were held by the</p>\n<h4 id=\"commission-in-geneva\">Commission in Geneva:</h4>\n<p>• The situation of human rights in the territories of former Yugoslavia 13 - 14</p>\n<p>August 1992.</p>\n<p>• The situation of human rights in the territories of former Yugoslavia, 30th</p>\n<p>November - 1 December 1992.</p>\n<p>• The situation of human rights in Rwanda 24 -25 May 1994</p>\n<p>• The situation in East Timor, 23 - 27 September 1999</p>\n<p>• Grave and massive violations of human rights of the Palestinian people by</p>\n<p>Israel 17 - 19 October 2000.</p>\n<p>1.14 Other Treaty Bodies Dealing with Human Rights Issues</p>\n<p>There are other treaty based bodies under the United Nations system, which are</p>\n<p>charged with the responsibility of supervising implementation of human rights</p>\n<p>treaties. They are referred to as treaty based because they are established under</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-relevant-treaties-or-conventions-dealing-with-human-rights-these-include\">the relevant treaties or conventions dealing with human rights. These include:</h4>\n<p>The Committee Against Torture, The Committee on Economic, Social and</p>\n<p>Cultural Rights, The Committee on the Elimination of All forms of</p>\n<p>Discrimination Against Women, The Committee on the Elimination of All</p>\n<p>Forms of Racial Discrimination, The Committee on the Rights of the Child.</p>\n<p>2.14.1 The Committee Against Torture</p>\n<p>The Committee was established in terms of article 17 of the Convention Against</p>\n<p>Torture and Other Cruel Inhuman and Degrading Treatment or Punishment.</p>\n<p>The purpose of the Committee is to supervise implementation of the Convention</p>\n<p>Against Torture.</p>\n<p>The Committee comprises 10 experts of high moral standing and recognized</p>\n<p>competence in the field of human rights. They serve in their individual capacity.</p>\n<p>The experts are elected by states parties for a term of 4 years. They are eligible</p>\n<p>for re-election if re-nominated. States parties are responsible for the expenses of</p>\n<p>the Committee, while they perform the duties of the Committee. The Committee</p>\n<p>meets in two sessions each year in Geneva.</p>\n<p>The main working documents of the Committee are issued under the series</p>\n<p>symbol CAT/C/-, while the summary records of meetings are issued under series</p>\n<p>symbol CAT/C/SR -, with a number at the end.</p>\n<p>Under Article 19 of the Convention, states parties are required to submit to the</p>\n<p>Committee through the Secretary General of the United Nations an initial report</p>\n<p>on the measures taken to give effect to the undertakings under the Convention.</p>\n<p>This should be one year after entry into force for the states party concerned and</p>\n<p>thereafter every 4 years. A list of these reports may be accessed in the UN-I-</p>\n<p>QUE data base.</p>\n<p>2.14.2 The Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights</p>\n<p>The Committee was established by ECOSOC resolution 1985/17. Its main</p>\n<p>function is to supervise implementation of the International Covenant on</p>\n<p>Economic, Social and Cultural Rights. Its working documents are issued under</p>\n<p>the series symbol E/C.12/--, while the summary records are documented under</p>\n<p>the series E/C/--SR. States parties are enjoined by ECOSOC Resolution 1988/4</p>\n<p>to submit an initial report on the measures adopted and progress made in</p>\n<p>implementing the rights contained in the Covenant, within two years of entry</p>\n<p>into force for the states party concerned and thereafter every 5 years. Reports</p>\n<p>may be accessed in the UN-I-QUE data- base.</p>\n<p>2.14.3 The Committee on the Elimination of All Discrimination Against</p>\n<p>Women</p>\n<p>The Committee is provided for under article 17 of the Convention on the</p>\n<p>Elimination of All Discrimination Against Women.</p>\n<p>The Committee is the supervisory organ of the Convention. It makes sure that</p>\n<p>states parties to the convention implement the obligations contained there in.</p>\n<p>The Committee comprises 23 experts of high moral standing and competence</p>\n<p>covered by the Convention. The experts are elected by states parties from among</p>\n<p>their nationals and they serve in their personal capacity. Equitable geographical</p>\n<p>distribution is considered as well as the different forms of civilizations, including</p>\n<p>the principle legal systems. The members are elected for a term of 4 years.</p>\n<p>Emoluments of this Committee are met by the United Nations.</p>\n<p>The main working documents are issued under the series symbol CEDAW and</p>\n<p>the current status of ratification is posted on the website for the Division for the</p>\n<p>Advancement of Women (DAW).</p>\n<p>The Committee meets in two sessions each year in Mew York. The summary</p>\n<p>record of meetings are issued under the symbol CEDAW/C/SR.</p>\n<p>2.14.4 The Committee on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial</p>\n<p>Discrimination</p>\n<p>The Committee was established pursuant to article 8 of the International</p>\n<p>Covenant on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination.</p>\n<p>The purpose of the Committee is to supervise the implementation of the</p>\n<p>Convention. The Committee meets in two sessions each year in Geneva.</p>\n<p>The Committee consists of 18 experts, of high moral standing and acknowledged</p>\n<p>impartiality elected by states parties, from among their nationals. These experts</p>\n<p>serve in their personal capacity. Consideration is given to equitable geographical</p>\n<p>distribution and representation of the different forms of civilization as well as</p>\n<p>the principal legal systems. Members of the Committee are elected for 4 years.</p>\n<p>States parties are responsible for the expenses of the members of the Committee,</p>\n<p>while they perform their duties.</p>\n<p>States parties to the Convention are required to submit to the Committee through</p>\n<p>the Secretary General of the United Nations, a report on the legislative, judicial</p>\n<p>and administrative measures, which they have adopted which give effect to the</p>\n<p>provisions of the Convention, within 1 year after the Convention enters into</p>\n<p>force in the state parties jurisdiction. They are there after required to furnish a</p>\n<p>report every 2 years.</p>\n<p>The working documents of the Committee are issued under the series symbol</p>\n<p>CERD/C. The summary records of meetings are issued under the series symbol</p>\n<h2 id=\"cerd-c-sr\">CERD/C/SR.</h2>\n<p>2.14.5 The Committee on the Rights of the Child</p>\n<p>The Committee on the Rights of the Child was issued pursuant to article 43 of</p>\n<p>the Convention on the Rights of the Child. Its main purpose is to supervise the</p>\n<p>Convention on the Rights of the Child.</p>\n<p>The Committee comprises 10 experts of high moral standing and recognized</p>\n<p>competence in the area of the Convention. The members are elected by states</p>\n<p>parties from their nationals, and serve in their personal capacity. Equitable</p>\n<p>geographical distribution and the principal legal systems are factor taken into</p>\n<p>considerations when members are elected. The members are elected for a term</p>\n<p>of 4 years. The Committee meets in 3 sessions each year in Geneva</p>\n<p>The parties to the Convention are required in terms of article 44 to submit an</p>\n<p>initial report on the measures adopted, which give effect to the rights recognized</p>\n<p>under the covenant and to document progress made in the enjoyment of those</p>\n<p>rights within 2 years of the Convention’s entry into force for the states party</p>\n<p>concerned, and thereafter every 5 years.</p>\n<p>The working documents are issued under the series symbol CRC/C/-, while the</p>\n<p>summary records are issued under the series symbol CRC/C/SR. For details on</p>\n<p>reports see UN-I-QUE data base.</p>\n<p>2.15 Specialised Agencies of the United Nations</p>\n<p>2.16.1 International Labour Organisation (Establishment)</p>\n<p>The International Labour organization, whose office is located in Geneva,</p>\n<p>Switzerland, was founded in 1919, by the Treaty of Versailles. It is therefore</p>\n<p>considered as the first organization to be accorded the status of the specialized</p>\n<p>agency of the United Nations, under an agreement with ECOSOC in 1945.</p>\n<p>2.16.1 (a) The Structure of ILO</p>\n<p>This organization has a peculiar structure, when compared with the rest of the</p>\n<p>other international organizations. Although it is an inter-governmental</p>\n<p>organization, its decision-making bodies are tripartite, which comprises</p>\n<p>representatives of workers and employers as well as government.</p>\n<p>Each member state is required to send 2 representatives to the annual</p>\n<p>international labour conference. The Most Representative Organization of</p>\n<p>Employers and Workers also send one representative each. See article 3 of the</p>\n<p>ILO Constitution. This means that in the governing body of the International</p>\n<p>Labour Office, governments have 50 percent of the votes, employers 25 percent,</p>\n<p>and workers another 25 percent. This arrangement makes ILO the only inter-</p>\n<p>governmental body in which non-governmental organizations have a significant</p>\n<p>role to play in decision-making.</p>\n<p>2.16.1 (b) The International Labour Standards and Human Rights</p>\n<p>Issues</p>\n<p>The motivating factor behind the creation of the International Labour</p>\n<p>Organisation was the need to adopt international standards and to improve the</p>\n<p>situation of workers. To this end ILO adopts conventions binding on those</p>\n<p>countries, which ratify them. In addition to this ILO makes recommendations,</p>\n<p>which have the force of declarations and provide guidelines on the best practice</p>\n<p>as far as labour and related matters are concerned. Since it came into existence</p>\n<p>in 1919, ILO had as of the year 2000 adopted 183 conventions, which have been</p>\n<p>ratified by very many states. ILO has also contributed 190 recommendations.</p>\n<p>As far as ILO’s guiding principle expressed in its constitution is concerned,</p>\n<p>lasting universal peace can only be established if it is based on social justice.28</p>\n<p>It is for this reason that ILO seeks to improve the conditions of workers.</p>\n<p>Guided by the same principle, ILO in the Declaration of Philadelphia took</p>\n<h4 id=\"steps-to-advance-human-rights-by-proclaiming-that\">steps to advance human rights by proclaiming that:</h4>\n<p>“All human beings, irrespective of race, creed or sex, have the right</p>\n<p>to          pursue their material well being and he spiritual development</p>\n<p>in          conditions of freedom and dignity of economic security and</p>\n<p>equal        opportunity”.</p>\n<p>Following this declaration the ILO was in the next 12 years involved in a series</p>\n<p>of standard setting exercise. These standards formed a basis for the labour related</p>\n<p>provisions in two human rights covenants, namely the International Covenant</p>\n<p>on Civil and Political Rights and the International Covenant on Economic, Social</p>\n<p>and Cultural Rights of 1966.</p>\n<p>What the ILO considers as its fundamental human rights standards are those that</p>\n<p>covere´4 areas namely: Freedom of Association and Protection of the right to</p>\n<p>organize, Freedom from Forced Labour, Child Labour, and Discrimination</p>\n<p>28</p>\n<p>See the Preamble to the ILO Constitution.</p>\n<p>There are 8 conventions which embody what ILO calls the Core Principles.</p>\n<h4 id=\"these-are\">These are:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-freedom-od-association-and-the-rght-to-organise-1948-no-87\">1. Freedom od Association and the Rght to Organise, 1948 (No 87)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-right-to-organise-and-collective-bargaining-1949-no-98\">2. Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining, 1949, (No 98)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-forced-labour-1930-no-29\">3. Forced Labour, 1930, (No.29)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-abolition-of-forced-labour-1957-no-105\">4. Abolition of Forced Labour, 1957 (No 105)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-equal-remuneration-1950-no-100\">5. Equal Remuneration, 1950, (No.100)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"6-discrimination-employment-and-occupation-1958-no-111\">6. Discrimination (Employment and Occupation) 1958 (No 111)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"7-minimum-age-1973-no-138\">7. Minimum Age, 1973 (No 138)</h3>\n<h3 id=\"8-worst-forms-of-child-labour-1999-no-182\">8. Worst Forms of Child Labour 1999, (No 182)</h3>\n<p>2.16.1 (c) Supervision of the ILO Human Rights Standards</p>\n<p>Under article 22 of the ILO Constitution, a government is required to send</p>\n<p>periodic reports to the International Labour Office. The government is also</p>\n<p>required by article 23 (2) to send copies of the reports to the most representative</p>\n<p>organization of employers and workers in the country. The reports are examined</p>\n<p>by a Committee of Experts on the Application of the Conventions and</p>\n<p>Recommendations. This Committee comprises 20 independent experts drawn</p>\n<p>from all economic and social systems. The Committee makes comments on the</p>\n<p>report and the passed on to the International Labour Congress, where the report</p>\n<p>is discussed by a Tripartite Conference Committee on the Application of</p>\n<p>Standards. The Committee chooses certain observations for discussion and</p>\n<p>invites the concerned governments to appear before it to respond</p>\n<p>Under article 24, the Organization of Employers and Workers may make a</p>\n<p>submission that a government has not taken measures to ensure the satisfactory</p>\n<p>observance of the convention, which the government has ratified.</p>\n<p>2.17 (a) UNESCO (Establishment and Purpose)</p>\n<p>The Constitution of the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural</p>\n<p>Organisation was adopted in 1945.</p>\n<p>The purpose of UNESCO is set out in Article 1 of the Constitution, which</p>\n<h4 id=\"provides-inter-alia\">provides inter alia:</h4>\n<p>“To contribute to peace and security by promoting collaboration among the</p>\n<p>nations through education, science and culture in order to further</p>\n<p>universal             respect for justice, for the rule of law and for the human</p>\n<p>rights and fundamental             freedoms which are affirmed for the people’s</p>\n<p>of the world… by the Charter               of the United Nations”.</p>\n<p>UNESCO’s main concern in the area of human rights covers right to education,</p>\n<p>freedom of expression, and the rights relating to culture, arts and science.</p>\n<p>Initially UNESCO was of the view that it had no power to take action on</p>\n<p>complaints made to it about violations of human rights. This changed when it</p>\n<p>adopted in 1978 a new procedure for considering such communications. The</p>\n<p>Executive Board of UNESCO made a decision, which inter alia resolved that the</p>\n<p>Committee be designated as the Committee on Conventions and</p>\n<p>Recommendations. It was also decided that the Committee would continue to</p>\n<p>carry out its functions with respect to the conventions and recommendations</p>\n<p>and would consider communication received by UNESCO concerning cases and</p>\n<p>questions of violations of human rights, within UNESCO’s field of competence.</p>\n<p>2.17 (b) UNESCO’s Criteria to Accept Communication</p>\n<p>In order for a communication to be considered admissible, such a</p>\n<p>communication must not be anonymous. It must come from a person or group</p>\n<p>of persons, who can be reasonably presumed to be victims, or from quarters</p>\n<p>having reliable knowledge of an alleged violation of human rights within the</p>\n<p>competence of UNESCO.</p>\n<p>The Communication must be compatible with the principles of UNESCO, the</p>\n<p>Charter of the United Nations and other instruments ratified in the area of</p>\n<p>human rights.</p>\n<p>Any communication which may be adjudged to be manifestly ill founded,</p>\n<p>offensive, based exclusively on information obtained through the mass media,</p>\n<p>presented beyond reasonable time limit and where the domestic remedies</p>\n<p>available to the victim are not exhausted, can-not be entertained by</p>\n<h2 id=\"unesco\">UNESCO.</h2>\n<h4 id=\"the-following-have-been-observed-to-be-within-unesco-s-area-of-competence\">The following have been observed to be within UNESCO’s area of competence:</h4>\n<p>the right to education, the right to share in scientific advancement and enjoy its</p>\n<p>benefits, the right to participate freely in cultural life, the right to information,</p>\n<p>including freedom of opinion and expression.</p>\n<p>2.17 (c) UNESCO’s Procedure and to Accept a Communication</p>\n<p>UNESCO’s procedure of examining cases is confidential and takes place in</p>\n<p>private       meetings     of    The Committee on          Conventions</p>\n<p>and</p>\n<p>Recommendation, which is one of the permanent subsidiary bodies of the</p>\n<p>executive board. Presently it comprises 30 members. UNESCO’s procedure</p>\n<p>contrasts sharply with the procedure adopted by the Treaty bodies responsible</p>\n<p>for supervising implementation of the treaties/conventions.29</p>\n<p>2.18 Food and Agricultural Organisation</p>\n<p>The Food and Agricultural Organisation was founded in October 1945, with a</p>\n<p>mandate to raise the levels of nutrition and standard of living. It was also saddled</p>\n<p>with the responsibility to improve agricultural production and better living</p>\n<p>conditions of rural populations. FAO’s constitution was amended in 1965 to</p>\n<p>include a provision on ensuring humanity’s freedom from hunger.</p>\n<p>FAO is the largest specialized agency of the United Nations, having 180 member</p>\n<p>nations. The European Union is a member of the organization. FAO’s main field</p>\n<p>concern extends to involvement in land and water development, plant and animal</p>\n<p>production, forestry, fisheries, economic and social policy, investment,</p>\n<p>sustainable development, nutrition, food standards and commodities trade. The</p>\n<p>organization also plays a significant role in dealing with food and agricultural</p>\n<p>emergencies.</p>\n<p>29</p>\n<p>The reports are considered in public meetings.</p>\n<p>As far as human rights are concerned, FAO has contributed significantly to the</p>\n<p>increased awareness of the right to food. For example refer to the Rome</p>\n<p>Declaration on World Food Security and Plan of Action, adopted by the</p>\n<p>World Food Summit (WFS) in November 1996, which made many references</p>\n<p>to human rights generally and the right to adequate food and freedom from</p>\n<p>hunger in particular.30</p>\n<p>It is however worth pointing out that in practice FAO is more concerned with</p>\n<p>issues regarding the realization and enjoyment of the right to food than the</p>\n<p>violation of any other human rights, including the right to food. FAO feels that</p>\n<p>other for a such as the Human Rights Commission and the Office of the High</p>\n<p>Commissioner for Human Rights are better suited to look at violations.</p>\n<p>2.19 The United Nations Development Programme (UNDP)</p>\n<p>This is the development agency of the United Nations. Development in the</p>\n<p>context of the United Nations is wide ranging. According to Mark Malloch</p>\n<p>Brown, the UNDP Administrator, the organization concerns itself with issues</p>\n<p>covering the area of politics, security, public health, crime prevention, and</p>\n<p>environment, because these issues can no longer be handled by a single nation,</p>\n<p>hence the United Nations’ endeavour to bring together governments, civil</p>\n<p>society, multinational corporations and multilateral organizations to work around</p>\n<p>particular issues of concern, and to find solutions to them. The following are the</p>\n<p>areas, where UNDP is active: democratic governance, poverty reduction, crisis</p>\n<p>prevention and recovery, energy and environment, information and</p>\n<p>communication technologies, HIV/AIDS, and processing and production of</p>\n<p>human development report.</p>\n<p>2.19 (a) Democratic Governance</p>\n<p>UNDP is actively involved in bringing people together in nations to build</p>\n<p>partnerships and to share ways to promote participation, accountability and</p>\n<p>effectiveness at all levels. The organization helps countries to strengthen electoral</p>\n<p>and legislative system, improve access to justice and public administration and</p>\n<p>develop a greater capacity to deliver basic services to those in most need. This</p>\n<p>30</p>\n<p>See report of World Food Summit Part one, FAO, Rome 1997: Rome Declaration on World Food</p>\n<p>Security and World Food Summit Plan of Action.</p>\n<p>contributes to the realization of the right to political participation and judicial</p>\n<p>rights.</p>\n<p>2.19 (b) Poverty Reduction</p>\n<p>UNDP is working closely with national governments to eradicate poverty, but</p>\n<p>UNDP insists on strategies based on local needs and priorities. UNDP sponsors</p>\n<p>innovative pilot projects, encourages adoption of best practices and resources,</p>\n<p>promote the role of women in development, and brings governments, civil</p>\n<p>societies and donors together and coordinate their efforts. The efforts are</p>\n<p>intended to promote and realize the right of every one to an adequate</p>\n<p>standard of living for an individual and his/her family, including the right</p>\n<p>to food, clothing and housing and to the continuous improvement of living</p>\n<p>condition, as set out in Article 11 of the International Covenant on Economic,</p>\n<p>Social and Cultural Rights of 1966.</p>\n<p>2.19 (c) Crisis Prevention and Recovery</p>\n<p>UNDP is involved in preventing crisis and natural disasters, which may blot out</p>\n<p>development and increase poverty and inequality. Therefore, UNDP through its</p>\n<p>world-wide-network shares innovative approaches to crisis prevention and early</p>\n<p>warning and conflict resolution. These efforts are intended to enhance the</p>\n<p>enjoyment and realization of the right to development generally.</p>\n<p>2.19 (d) Energy and Environment</p>\n<p>UNDP helps countries to strengthen their capacities to address environmental</p>\n<p>degradation at the national level. This issue is of a global concern and has a direct</p>\n<p>bearing on the realization of the right to a clean environment.</p>\n<p>2.19 (e) Information and Communication Technology</p>\n<p>UNDP helps countries to benefit from the best practices around the world to</p>\n<p>develop strategies, which expand access to ICT and use it in the development</p>\n<p>process. This is instrumental in the realization of the right to development</p>\n<p>2.20 (f) HIV/AIDS</p>\n<p>UNDP works with national governments to prevent the spread of HIV/AIDS</p>\n<p>and to reduce its impact UNDP advocates placing HIV/AIDS at the centre of</p>\n<p>national planning and budgets. It helps build national capacity to manage</p>\n<p>initiatives that include people and institutions not usually involved with public</p>\n<p>health. This is a deliberate effort to help countries realize the right to health.</p>\n<p>2.21 Human Development Report</p>\n<p>UNDP works extensively in advocating and preparing human development</p>\n<p>reports. These focus on key development issues, providing measurement tools</p>\n<p>and innovative analysis. UNDP is guided by the belief that development is</p>\n<p>ultimately a process of enlarging people’s choices, not only raising national</p>\n<p>incomes. In preparing these development reports UNDP draws on a world-wide-</p>\n<p>network of leaders from the academic institutions, government and civil society.</p>\n<p>These reports are crucial for development, hence it may be said that they in a</p>\n<p>way contribute to the realization of the right to development. As of April 2003</p>\n<p>420 National Development Reports had been published in 135 countries of the</p>\n<p>world.</p>\n<p>2.22 The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees</p>\n<p>We will not go into details because this is adequately covered under the relevant</p>\n<p>course.</p>\n<p>According to Chapter 1 paragraph 1 of the ….., The United Nations High</p>\n<p>Commissioner for Refugees, acting under the authority of the General Assembly,</p>\n<p>shall assume the function of providing international protection, under the</p>\n<p>auspices of the United Nations, to refugees who fall within the scope of the</p>\n<p>present statute and of seeking permanent solutions for the problem of refugees</p>\n<p>by assisting governments and subject to the approval of the governments</p>\n<p>concerned, private organizations to facilitate the voluntary repatriation of such</p>\n<p>refugees, or to their assimilation with new national communities.</p>\n<p>In the exercise of his/her functions, more particularly when difficulties arise, and</p>\n<p>for instance with regard to any controversy concerning the international status</p>\n<p>of these persons, the High Commissioner shall request the opinion of an advisory</p>\n<p>committee on refugees if it is created.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 provides that the work of the High Commissioner shall be of an</p>\n<p>entirely non political character; It shall be humanitarian and social and shall relate</p>\n<p>as a rule, to groups and categories of refugees. In executing the duties of his/her</p>\n<p>office, the High Commissioner is bound to follow directives issued by the</p>\n<p>General Assembly and or ECOSOC.</p>\n<p>In short the office of the High Commissioner of Refugees is there to ensure that</p>\n<p>the rights of the refugees are not violated and as and when this happens his/her</p>\n<p>office intervenes.</p>\n<p>2.23 The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, 1966</p>\n<p>After the declaration of the UDHR, it was evident that the UDHR did not</p>\n<p>impose sufficient binding obligations to the member states of the United</p>\n<p>Nations, but as mentioned above it was more of a moral and political instrument,</p>\n<p>meant to be a standard setting instrument. The United Nations Commission on</p>\n<p>Human Rights then proceeded to draft covenants intended to be legally binding</p>\n<p>on the members of the UN, that have ratified these covenants.</p>\n<p>The drafting work started in 1947 and was completed in 1954. Due to political</p>\n<p>and ideological differences the covenants were only adopted by the United</p>\n<p>Nations General Assembly in 1966. It came into force on 23 March 1976.</p>\n<p>According to Moira Rayner31, this instrument is considered as one of the most</p>\n<p>important instrument for protection of all human rights. This was further</p>\n<p>reiterated in 1998, when China adopted it.</p>\n<p>When the state signs and ratifies the ICCPR, such a state is guaranteeing to</p>\n<p>protect all rights it covers. These rights belong to individuals within the territory</p>\n<p>of the member state, whatever their status, irrespective of their sex, race,</p>\n<p>nationality, beliefs, social or economic standing, and without distinction or</p>\n<p>discrimination.</p>\n<p>31</p>\n<p>See……………….</p>\n<p>The signatories promise to provide an effective remedy for violation of any right</p>\n<p>protected in the covenant. As the title suggests, the covenant protects civil and</p>\n<p>political rights, which include the right to life, although it allows capital</p>\n<p>punishment in some instances; the right to liberty; freedom from arbitrary arrest</p>\n<p>and detention; it guarantees humane treatment if detention or imprisonment is</p>\n<p>according to law; it provides that juveniles must not be jailed with adults; it</p>\n<p>prohibits torture, inhuman and degrading treatment; prohibits slavery or forced</p>\n<p>labour; guarantees freedom to move around and choose where to live; fair trial;</p>\n<p>the right to privacy; - family, home, at home, or correspondence; prohibits</p>\n<p>unlawful attacks on a person’s reputation or honour; guarantees freedom of</p>\n<p>thought, conscience, and religion; prohibits incitement to war or racial or</p>\n<p>religious hatred; guarantees the right to peaceful assembly, which should only be</p>\n<p>restricted in interest of safety, health, or other freedoms; freedom of association,</p>\n<p>including joining trade unions; the right to marry a person of one’s choice; the</p>\n<p>right to take part in public affairs including voting; the right of religious or</p>\n<p>linguistic minorities to preserve their culture; and the right to self-determination.</p>\n<p>Due to time constraints we will not look at the scope and content of each right.</p>\n<p>It would therefore be useful for the students to look at the general comments by</p>\n<p>the United Nations Human Rights Committee and the guidelines for reporting</p>\n<p>under the ICCPR, pertaining to various rights. The jurisprudence of the</p>\n<p>European Court of Human Rights and the Inter American system are crucial in</p>\n<p>this regard. We will later briefly look at a few of these rights, but let us look at</p>\n<p>the Optional Protocals.</p>\n<p>The Optional Protocol</p>\n<p>The Second Optional Protocol</p>\n<p>The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights</p>\n<p>Some of the Rights Covered under ICCPR and ICESCR</p>\n<p>The Right to Life</p>\n<p>The right to life is very important because with out this right the other right may</p>\n<p>not be realized. This has been well articulated by Professor Sieghart when he</p>\n<p>says</p>\n<p>As human rights can only attach to a living human being, one</p>\n<p>might expect the right to life itself to be in some sense</p>\n<p>primary,</p>\n<p>since none of the other rights would have any value or utility</p>\n<p>without it.</p>\n<p>He however, quickly points out that international instruments do not in fact</p>\n<p>accord it any formal primacy and that on the contrary, the International</p>\n<p>Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, European Convention on Human Rights,</p>\n<p>and the American Convention on Human Rights, all contain qualifications</p>\n<p>rendering it less than absolute and allow human rights to be deliberately</p>\n<p>terminated in certain specified cases.</p>\n<p>Professor Sieghart further notes that the right to life stands in marked contrast</p>\n<p>to some of the other rights protected by the same instrument, for example, the</p>\n<p>freedom from torture and other ill treatment, and freedom from slavery and</p>\n<p>servitude are both absolute and subject to no exceptions of any kind. He observes</p>\n<p>that it may therefore be said that international human rights law assigns a higher</p>\n<p>value to the quality of living as a process, than to the existence of life as a state.</p>\n<p>Before we proceed further, it is important for us to establish, when life begins.</p>\n<p>There are 2 major schools of thought.</p>\n<p>The first one is of the view that life begins at the moment of conception, and</p>\n<p>that any tampering with the fetus should be treated as terminating life. This view</p>\n<p>is mainly subscribed to by the religious communities, particularly the</p>\n<p>Catholic Church. One proponent of this school is Jan Lagman, who says</p>\n<p>The development of a human being begins with</p>\n<p>fertilization,    a process by which two highly specialized cells,</p>\n<p>the spermatozoon         from the male and the oocyte from the</p>\n<p>female unite to give rise to               to a new organism, the zygote32</p>\n<p>.</p>\n<p>The second school of thought is of the view that life begins at leas 6 or 8 weeks</p>\n<p>after conception. According to Silver Lee, a number of specialists working in the</p>\n<p>field of human reproduction have suggested that we stop using the word embryo</p>\n<p>to describe the developing entity that exists for the first two weeks after</p>\n<p>fertilization. He suggests that there is a need to adopt the word pre embryo. He</p>\n<p>further notes that that the term pre embryo has been embraced wholeheartedly,</p>\n<p>by some people for reasons that are political and not scientific. He further</p>\n<p>observes that the term is used to provide an illusion that there is something</p>\n<p>profoundly different between what we non medical biologists call a six day old</p>\n<p>embryo and what we and every one else call a sixteen day old embryo.</p>\n<p>That the term embryo is useful in a political arena, where decisions are made</p>\n<p>about whether to allow early embryo now called pre embryo experimentation as</p>\n<p>well as in the confines of a doctors office, where it can be used to allay moral</p>\n<p>fears that may arise. That the doctor ma as well tell those that are concerned that</p>\n<p>they should not worry, after all, it is only pre embryos that are being manipulated</p>\n<p>or are frozen, and that they wont turn into real human embryos, until after they</p>\n<p>are put back into the body. For further information about the right to life refer</p>\n<p>to the Human Rights</p>\n<p>Committee, General Comment 6, Article 6 (Sixteenth session, 1982.33</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee pronounced itself on the right to life in the case</p>\n<p>of Chitat Ng v Canada34, where Chitat Ng, a British subject born in Hong Kong</p>\n<p>and a resident of the United States of America, was convicted in</p>\n<p>Canada, in 1985, for attempted theft and shooting a security guard. In 1987, USA</p>\n<p>requested his extradition to the USA in order for him to stand trial in California</p>\n<p>on 19 counts. These counts included kidnapping and 12 murders committed</p>\n<p>between 1948 and 1985. If convicted there was a possibility of the imposition</p>\n<p>of the death penalty. At the time of submission of the communication, Chitat</p>\n<p>32</p>\n<p>See Jan Langman, Medical Embryology, 3rd edition, Baltimore, Williams and Wilkins, 1975, p3. See US</p>\n<p>Catholic Bishops Prolife Activities What is an embryo http………………………………….</p>\n<p>33</p>\n<p>See Compilation of General Comments and Recommendations Adopted by Human Rights Treaty Bodies,</p>\n<p>http//www1.unmn.edu/humanrights/gencomm/hrcom6.htm (University of Minnesota Human Rights</p>\n<p>Library.</p>\n<p>34</p>\n<p>Communication to the Humana Rights Committee No. 469/1991.</p>\n<p>was detained in Canada, which had abolished the death penalty in 1976, with a</p>\n<p>limited exception for certain military offences. Chitat’s lawyer argued that if his</p>\n<p>client (the author of the communication) was extradited to the USA, the death</p>\n<p>penalty would be imposed on him. He further argued that this penalty was against</p>\n<p>the right to life and is cruel, degrading treatment.</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee inter alia observed that Mr. Chitat Ng was not a</p>\n<p>victim of a violation by Canada of Article 6 of the ICCPR. However, the</p>\n<p>Committee provided guidelines to be followed in determining whether in a</p>\n<p>particular case the imposition of the capital punishment constituted what is</p>\n<p>prohibited under Article 7 of the ICCPR. The Committee will have regard to the</p>\n<p>relevant personal factors regarding the author of the communication, the specific</p>\n<p>conditions of detention on death row, and whether the proposed method of</p>\n<p>execution is particularly abhorrent. In this case it was contended that execution</p>\n<p>by gas asphyxiation is contrary to internationally accepted standards of humane</p>\n<p>treatment of article 7 of the covenant</p>\n<p>Similarly, the European Court of Human Rights held in Soering v UK35, that</p>\n<p>extradition of Soering, a German national wanted by a Virginia Court in the</p>\n<p>USA, for murder, would amount to breach of Article 3 of the European</p>\n<p>Convention, which prohibits torture or inhuman or degrading treatment or</p>\n<p>punishment.</p>\n<p>The South African Constitutional Court also took a similar position in State v</p>\n<p>Makhoanyane36. In this case Makwanyane and others, who were the appellants,</p>\n<p>had been convicted of murder and sentenced to death by the Witwatersrand</p>\n<p>Local Division of the Supreme Court. 11 judges were unanimous that the death</p>\n<p>penalty was unconstitutional, because they argued inter alia, that the death</p>\n<p>penalty infringed the right to life.</p>\n<p>The dissenting opinion and partially dissenting opinion in the case of Open Door</p>\n<p>and Dublin Well Women v Ireland47, also offers useful jurisprudence on the right</p>\n<p>to life of an unborn life.</p>\n<p>35</p>\n<p>(1989 11 EHRR 439 (European Human Rights Reports)</p>\n<p>36</p>\n<h2 id=\"cct-3-94-1995-1-lrc-269\">CCT 3 94, 1995 1 LRC 269</h2>\n<p>Freedom from Torture, Cruel and Inhuman Treatment</p>\n<p>The ICCPR provided for this right under article 7. It says that no one shall be</p>\n<p>subjected to torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment. In</p>\n<p>particular no one shall be subjected without his consent to medical or scientific</p>\n<p>experimentation. Torture is not defined in the ICCPR. We have to refer to article</p>\n<p>1 of the Convention Against Torture, which provides that for purposes of this</p>\n<p>convention, the term torture means</p>\n<p>or mental is intentionally inflicted</p>\n<p>“any act by which severe pain or suffering whether physical</p>\n<p>on a person for such purposes as obtaining from him or a third person</p>\n<p>information or a confession, punishing        him for an act he or a third</p>\n<p>person has committed, or intimidating or coercing him or a third person</p>\n<p>or for any reason based on discrimination of any kind, when such pain</p>\n<p>or suffering is inflicted by or at the instigation of or with the consent</p>\n<p>or acquiescence of a public official or other person acting in an official</p>\n<p>capacity. It does not include pain or suffering arising only from</p>\n<p>inherent in or incidental to lawful sanctions”</p>\n<p>Torture can not be permitted under any circumstance whatsoever. This is further</p>\n<p>attested to by the decision of the European Court of Human Rights in the case</p>\n<p>of Ireland v UK48, where the court found that the conduct of the British Security</p>\n<p>forces in Northern Ireland constituted in human and degrading treatment, but</p>\n<p>there was a disagreement on whether such a conduct constituted torture.</p>\n<p>The European Court also confirmed that existence of an objective such as</p>\n<p>obtaining of information or confession is a necessary ingredient for torture to</p>\n<p>exist, however, the dissenting opinion in the case of Ireland v UK, Judge</p>\n<p>47</p>\n<p>29 10 1992. This and other cases may be accessed on www.HRNI</p>\n<p>48</p>\n<p>See…………..</p>\n<p>Fitzmaurice observed that the motivation for torture is irrelevant. He was of</p>\n<p>the view that torture is torture whatever its purpose, if inflicted compulsorily.</p>\n<p>In Denmark v Greece, it was held that solitary confinement, isolation in a police</p>\n<p>cell without food, water or access to toilets, mock execution, a threat to throw</p>\n<p>the detainee out of the window, the use of insulting language, rubbing the head</p>\n<p>of the detainee with vomit, stripping the detainee naked, or forcing the detainee</p>\n<p>to be present at the torture or inhumane treatment of relatives or friends,</p>\n<p>sometimes overcrowded detention quarters, lack of proper washing facilities,</p>\n<p>unsatisfactory dental treatment, restriction of mail.</p>\n<p>In Selmouni v France 37, The European Court of Human Rights also pronounced</p>\n<p>itself on the issue of torture and ill-treatment, while in custody. In this case</p>\n<p>Selmouni had been arrested on suspicion of drug trafficking. While in police</p>\n<p>custody, he was assaulted with a base ball bat, forced to knell before a young</p>\n<p>lady, forced to suck the private parts of a police man, before being urinated upon</p>\n<p>by the same police man. He was sodomised with a rubber truncheon, and</p>\n<p>threatened with burning of his feet. The court held that Selmouni had been</p>\n<p>tortured. See also Tomasi v France.</p>\n<p>Finally, before we leave this sub-topic, it would be useful to see what the United</p>\n<p>Nations Human Rights Committee has said about torture. In the case of Sergio</p>\n<p>Euben Lopez Burgos v Uruguay38, In this case……………..</p>\n<p>The Human Rights Committee in its General Comment 39, came up with details</p>\n<h4 id=\"on-the-provisions-of-article-7-of-the-iccpr-these-are-as-follows\">on the provisions of article 7 of the ICCPR. These are as follows:</h4>\n<p>11 To guarantee the effective protection of detained persons, provision should</p>\n<p>be made for the detainees to be held in places officially recognised as places of</p>\n<p>detention and for their names to be places of detention as well as for the names</p>\n<p>of the persons responsible for their detention, to be kept in registers readily</p>\n<p>available and accessible to the concerned, including relatives and friends. To the</p>\n<p>same effect, the time and place of all interrogations should be recorded, together</p>\n<p>with the names of all those present and this information should be recorded,</p>\n<p>together with names of all those present and this information should be available</p>\n<p>for purposes of judicial or administrative proceedings. Provision should also be</p>\n<p>made against incommunicado detention. In that connection, states parties should</p>\n<p>ensure that any place of detention be free from any equipment liable to be used</p>\n<p>for facilitating torture or ill-treatment. The protection of the detainees also</p>\n<p>37</p>\n<p>28/07/1999</p>\n<p>38</p>\n<p>Communication No. 12/52 (6th June 1979)</p>\n<p>39</p>\n<p>No 20, of 1992</p>\n<p>requires that prompt and regular access be given to doctors and lawyers and</p>\n<p>under appropriate supervision when the investigations so require, to family</p>\n<p>members.</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-it-is-important-for-the-discouragement-of-violations-under-article-7-that-the\">12. It is important for the discouragement of violations under article 7, that the</h3>\n<p>law must prohibit the use of admissibility in judicial proceedings of statements</p>\n<p>or confessions obtained through torture or other prohibited treatment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"15-the-committee-has-noted-that-some-states-have-granted-amnesty-in-respect\">15. The Committee has noted that some states have granted amnesty in respect</h3>\n<p>of acts of torture. Amnesties are generally incompatible with the duty of states</p>\n<p>to investigate such acts, to guarantee freedom from such acts within their</p>\n<p>jurisdictions; and to ensure that they do not occur in the future. States may not</p>\n<p>deprive individuals of the right to an effective remedy, including compensation</p>\n<p>and such full rehabilitation as may be possible.</p>\n<p>The Right to Health</p>\n<p>This right is protected by article 12 of the International Covenant On Economic,</p>\n<p>Social and Cultural Rights.???? In particular, paragraph 1 thereof provides that</p>\n<p>states parties to the present covenant recognise the right of every one to the</p>\n<p>enjoyment of the highest attainable standard of physical and mental health; while</p>\n<p>paragraph 2 states that the steps to be taken by states parties to the present</p>\n<p>covenant to achieve the full realisation of this right shall include those necessary</p>\n<h4 id=\"for\">for:</h4>\n<p>(a) The provision for the reduction of the rate of the still birth rate and of the</p>\n<p>mortality and health development of the child;</p>\n<p>(b) The improvement of all aspects of environmental and industrial hygiene;</p>\n<p>(c) The prevention, treatment and control of epidemics, endemic, occupational</p>\n<p>and other diseases;</p>\n<p>(d) The creation of conditions which would assure to all medical services and</p>\n<p>medical attention in the event of sickness.</p>\n<p>The Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights has in General</p>\n<h4 id=\"comment-number-14-elaborated-the-right-to-health-as-follows\">Comment Number 14 elaborated the right to health as follows:</h4>\n<p>Paragraph 1: Health is a fundamental human right indispensable for the exercise</p>\n<p>of other human rights. Every human being is entitled to the enjoyment of the</p>\n<p>highest attainable standard of health conducive to living a life in dignity. The</p>\n<p>realisation of the right to health may be pursued through numerous</p>\n<p>complementary approaches, such as the formulation of health policies, or</p>\n<p>implementation of health programmes developed by the World Health</p>\n<p>Organisation (WHO), or the adoption of specific legal instruments. Moreover,</p>\n<p>the right to health includes certain components which are legally enforceable.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3: The right to health is closely related to and depends upon the</p>\n<p>realisation of other human rights, as contained in the International Bill of Rights,</p>\n<p>including the right to food, housing, work, education, human dignity, life, non-</p>\n<p>discrimination, equality, prohibition against torture, privacy, access to</p>\n<p>information, and the freedom of Association, assembly and movement. These</p>\n<p>and other rights and freedoms address integral components of the right to health.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 4: The right to health is not to be understood as a right to be healthy.</p>\n<p>The right to health contains both freedoms and entitlements. The freedom</p>\n<p>includes: the right to control one’s health and body, including sexual and</p>\n<p>reproductive freedoms and the right to be free from interference, such as the</p>\n<p>right to be free from torture, non-consensual medical treatment and</p>\n<p>experimentation. By contrast the entitlements include: the right to a system of</p>\n<p>health protection, which provides equality of opportunity for people to enjoy the</p>\n<p>highest attainable level of health.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 9: The notion of the highest attainable standard of health in article</p>\n<p>12.1 takes into account both the individual’s biological and social economic</p>\n<p>preconditions and a state’s available resources. There are a number of aspects,</p>\n<p>which can not be addressed solely within the relationship between states and</p>\n<p>individuals; in particular good health can not be ensured by a state, nor can states</p>\n<p>provide protection against every possible cause of human ill-health. Thus genetic</p>\n<p>factors, individual susceptibility to ill health and the adoption of unhealthy or</p>\n<p>risky life styles may play an important role with respect to an individual’s health.</p>\n<p>Consequently the right to health must be understood as a right to the enjoyment</p>\n<p>of a variety of facilities, goods, services and conditions necessary for the</p>\n<p>realisation of the highest attainable standard of health.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 10: Since the adoption of the two international covenants in 1966, the</p>\n<p>world health situation has changed dramatically and the notion of health has</p>\n<p>undergone substantial changes and has also widened in scope. More</p>\n<p>determinants to health are being taken into consideration, such as resource</p>\n<p>distribution and gender differences. A wider definition of health also takes into</p>\n<p>account such socially related concerns as violence and armed conflict. Moreover,</p>\n<p>formerly unknown diseases, such as human immunodeficiency viruses and</p>\n<p>acquired immunodeficiency viruses and Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome</p>\n<p>(HIV/AIDS) and others that have become more wide spread such as cancer, as</p>\n<p>well as the rapid growth of the world’s population, have created new obstacles</p>\n<p>for the realisation of the right to health which need to be taken into account</p>\n<p>when interpreting article 12.Tere are more provisions of this General</p>\n<p>Comment.40</p>\n<p>The Right to Education</p>\n<p>See p 75 hand written notes</p>\n<p>The Third Generation of Human Rights</p>\n<p>Why are they called third generation of human rights? This category of human</p>\n<p>rights is also referred to by some scholars as solidarity rights. That is because</p>\n<p>they are not vested in individuals but in collective groups of individuals called</p>\n<p>peoples. These rights are sometimes called collective rights. These rights</p>\n<p>include the right to development, the right of self- determination, the right</p>\n<p>of sovereignty over natural resources, etc.</p>\n<p>40</p>\n<p>See www.cesr.org/ESCR/gencomment14.htm</p>\n<p>Professor Seighart is of the view collective rights are expressed in a form that</p>\n<p>is difficult to reconcile with any classical theory of human rights. He argues that</p>\n<p>these collective rights are to a large extent said to attach to “peoples” rather than</p>\n<p>to “persons” or individuals. He advances 3 reasons, which may prevent collective</p>\n<h4 id=\"rights-from-being-recognized-as-a-specific-category-of-human-rights-these-are\">rights from being recognized as a specific category of human rights. These are:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-there-is-no-generally-accepted-definition-of-what-constitutes-a-people\">1. There is no generally accepted definition of what constitutes “a people”,</h3>\n<p>yet clearly not every group within a state can be entitled to call itself a</p>\n<p>people and consequently claim the right of selfdetermination as a</p>\n<p>legitimate ground for seceding from the state. He further argues that there</p>\n<p>is no straightforward test to establish whether any given individual does</p>\n<p>or does not form a part of any particular people and so shares that peoples</p>\n<p>rights.41</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-it-is-easy-to-identify-the-state-or-its-government-and-public-authorities-but\">2. It is easy to identify the state or its government and public authorities, but</h3>\n<p>it is less easy to identify those obliged to respect and secure the rights of</p>\n<p>the peoples. For example – Who has the power to ensure for a people the</p>\n<p>right to dispose of its natural wealth and resources?54</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-abstract-concepts-have-in-the-past-posed-grave-dangers-to-the-enjoyment\">3. Abstract concepts have in the past posed grave dangers to the enjoyment</h3>\n<p>by individuals of their human rights and fundamental freedoms. Some of</p>\n<p>the gross violations of human rights have been carried out in the name of</p>\n<p>some inspiring abstraction, such as the one true faith, the nation, the state,</p>\n<p>the economy, etc and that the term people may not be an exception.42</p>\n<p>41</p>\n<p>A good example is the Nuremberg law which required that for one to be considered an Aryan, one had</p>\n<p>to produce baptismal certificates of 4 grand parents, otherwise he was excluded from the group. 54 Such a</p>\n<p>power may be spread over states, public and national and international bodies and many individuals.</p>\n<p>42</p>\n<p>Some states have used national security and public order. For further reading on Peoples see: Nasila</p>\n<p>Rembe: The system of Protection of Human Rights Under the African Charter on Human and Peoples</p>\n<p>Rights: Problems and Prospects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-111-30-regional-system43\">PART 111 30. REGIONAL SYSTEM43</h2>\n<p>There are 3 major regional human rights systems. These are the European, the</p>\n<p>Inter American and the African System. We will not go into details because of</p>\n<p>time constraints. However, students are encouraged to read on their own. There</p>\n<p>are other regional systems such as the Asian Human Rights Commission and the</p>\n<p>Arab system. These are still in embryonic stage, and as such may not be referred</p>\n<p>to as functional systems.</p>\n<p>As far as this course is concerned the African System is the most relevant to us</p>\n<p>in Africa, but it is relatively new. Its jurisprudence is not yet well developed hence</p>\n<p>we will start with the first two, particularly the European System, which has had</p>\n<p>the benefit of history. The Inter American System has also had a lot of experience</p>\n<p>over the years although its development is still behind that of the European</p>\n<p>System.</p>\n<p>3.1 The European Convention on Human Rights of 1950</p>\n<p>3.1.2 Historical Background</p>\n<p>The Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental</p>\n<p>Freedoms was drawn up within the Council of Europe. It was opened for</p>\n<p>signature in Rome on 4 November 1950 and entered into force in September</p>\n<h3 id=\"1953-the-european-convention-s-starting-point-was-the-1948-universal\">1953. The European Convention’s starting point was the 1948 Universal</h3>\n<p>Declaration of Human Rights.44 The Europeans sought to pursue the aims of</p>\n<p>43</p>\n<p>Refer to the reading material on this section in the part that has Reading List with Case Citation and</p>\n<p>Bibliographic Information, attached to the Course Outline. .</p>\n<p>44</p>\n<p>See The preamble to this Convention states as follows: Considering the UDHR proclaimed by the General</p>\n<p>Assembly of the United Nations on 10 December 1948, Considering that this Declaration aims at securing</p>\n<p>the Universal and effective recognition and observance the rights therein declared; Considering that the</p>\n<p>aims of the Council of Europe is the achievement of greater unity between its members and that one of the</p>\n<p>the Council of Europe through the maintenance and further realisation of human</p>\n<p>rights and fundamental freedoms. It is therefore said that the Convention was to</p>\n<p>represent the first steps for the collective enforcement of some of the rights set</p>\n<p>out in the Universal Declaration.</p>\n<p>In addition to laying down a set of civil and political rights and freedoms, the</p>\n<p>Convention put in place a mechanism for the enforcement of the obligations</p>\n<p>entered into by Contracting States. Three institutions were entrusted with this</p>\n<p>responsibility: the European Commission of Human Rights (set up in 1954), the</p>\n<p>European Court of Human Rights (set up in 1959) and the Committee of</p>\n<p>Ministers of the Council of Europe, the latter organ being composed of the</p>\n<p>Ministers of Foreign Affairs of the member States or their representatives.</p>\n<p>Initially, under the Convention complaints could be brought against States parties</p>\n<p>either by other Contracting States or by individual applicants (individuals, groups</p>\n<p>of individuals or non-governmental organisations). Recognition of the right of</p>\n<p>individual application was, however, optional and it could therefore be exercised</p>\n<p>only against those States which had accepted it (Protocol No. 11 to the</p>\n<p>Convention was subsequently to make its acceptance compulsory.</p>\n<p>The complaints were first the subject of a preliminary examination by the</p>\n<p>Commission, which determined their admissibility. Where an application was</p>\n<p>declared admissible, the Commission placed itself at the parties&#x27; disposal with a</p>\n<p>view to making a friendly settlement. If no settlement was forthcoming, it drew</p>\n<p>up a report establishing the facts and expressing an opinion on the merits of the</p>\n<p>case. The report was transmitted to the Committee of Ministers.</p>\n<p>Where the respondent State had accepted the compulsory jurisdiction of the</p>\n<p>Court, the Commission and/or any Contracting State concerned had a period of</p>\n<p>three months following the transmission of the report to the Committee of</p>\n<p>Ministers within which to bring the case before the Court for a final, binding</p>\n<p>methods by which that aim is to be pursued is the maintenance and further realization of        human rights</p>\n<p>and fundamental freedoms, reaffirming their profound belief in those fundamental freedoms, which are</p>\n<p>foundations of justice and peace in the world and are best maintained on the one hand by an effective political</p>\n<p>democracy and on the other by a common understanding and observance of</p>\n<p>human rights upon which they depend; Being resolved, as the governments of</p>\n<p>European countries, which are like minded and have common heritage of</p>\n<p>political traditions, ideas, freedom and the rule of law, to take the first steps for</p>\n<p>the collective enforcement of certain freedoms defined in the Universal</p>\n<p>Declaration have agreed as follows: Article 1: The High Contracting parties shall</p>\n<p>secure to every one within their jurisdiction the rights and freedoms defined in</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-1-of-this-convention\">section 1 of this Convention.</h3>\n<p>adjudication. Individuals were not entitled to bring their cases before the Court.</p>\n<p>If a case was not referred to the Court, the Committee of Ministers decided</p>\n<p>whether there had been a violation of the Convention and, if appropriate,</p>\n<p>awarded &quot;just satisfaction&quot; to the victim. The Committee of Ministers also had</p>\n<p>responsibility for supervising the execution of the Court’s judgments.</p>\n<p>3.1.3 The Later Developments</p>\n<p>Since the Convention’s entry into force in 1953, thirteen Protocols have been</p>\n<p>adopted. Protocols Nos. 1, 4, 6, 7, 12 (this Protocol will enter into force when</p>\n<p>ratified by ten Contracting States) and 13 added further rights and liberties to</p>\n<p>those guaranteed by the Convention, while Protocol No. 2 conferred on the</p>\n<p>Court the power to give advisory opinions. Protocol No. 9 enabled individual</p>\n<p>applicants to bring their cases before the Court subject to ratification by the</p>\n<p>respondent State and acceptance by a screening panel. Protocol No. 11</p>\n<p>restructured the enforcement machinery. The remaining Protocols concerned</p>\n<p>the organisation of and procedure before the institutions set up under the</p>\n<p>Convention.</p>\n<p>Since 1980 there has been an increase in the number of cases brought before the</p>\n<p>Convention institutions. This posed operational problems. The problem was</p>\n<p>compounded by the accession of new Contracting States from 1990. The number</p>\n<p>of applications registered annually with the Commission increased from 404 in</p>\n<p>1981 to 4,750 in 1997. By that year, the number of unregistered or provisional</p>\n<p>files opened each year in the Commission had risen to over 12,000. The Court’s</p>\n<p>statistics reflected a similar story, with the number of cases referred annually</p>\n<p>rising from 7 in 1981 to 119 in 1997.</p>\n<p>The ever increasing case-load prompted a lengthy debate on the necessity for a</p>\n<p>reform of the Convention supervisory machinery, resulting in the adoption of</p>\n<p>Protocol No. 11 to the Convention. The aim was to simplify the structure with</p>\n<p>a view to shortening the length of proceedings while strengthening the judicial</p>\n<p>character of the system by making it fully compulsory and abolishing the</p>\n<p>Committee of Ministers’ adjudicative role.</p>\n<p>Protocol No. 11, which came into force on 1 November 1998, replaced the</p>\n<p>existing, part-time Court and Commission by a single, full-time Court. For a</p>\n<p>transitional period of one year (until 31 October 1999) the Commission</p>\n<p>continued to deal with the cases which it had previously declared admissible.</p>\n<p>During the three years which followed the entry into force of Protocol No.</p>\n<p>11 the Court’s case-load grew at an unprecedented rate. The number of</p>\n<p>applications registered rose from 5,979 in 1998 to 13,858 in 2001, an increase of</p>\n<p>approximately 130%. Concerns about the Court’s capacity to deal with the</p>\n<p>growing volume of cases led to requests for additional resources and speculation</p>\n<p>about the need for further reform.</p>\n<p>A Ministerial Conference on Human Rights, held in Rome on 3 and 4</p>\n<p>November 2000 to mark the 50th anniversary of the opening of the Convention</p>\n<p>for signature, had initiated a process of reflection on reform of the system. In</p>\n<p>November 2002, as a follow-up to a Ministerial Declaration on &quot;the Court of</p>\n<p>Human Rights for Europe&quot;, the Ministers’ Deputies issued terms of reference to</p>\n<p>the Steering Committee for Human Rights (CDDH) to draw up a set of concrete</p>\n<p>and coherent proposals covering measures that could be implemented without</p>\n<p>delay and possible amendments to the Convention.</p>\n<p>This regional human rights instrument, which came into existence in 1950,</p>\n<p>having been promulgated by the Council of Europe45, came into force in 1953.</p>\n<p>This Convention has had many Protocols, which guarantee enjoyment more</p>\n<p>rights. For example Protocol No… which came into force on 18th May 1954,</p>\n<p>provides for the right to peaceful enjoyment of possession, the right to education,</p>\n<p>the democratic right of holding free periodic elections.</p>\n<p>45</p>\n<p>The Council of Europe is an International Organization. It is different from the European Council and the</p>\n<p>Council of the European Union.</p>\n<p>Protocol No. 4, which entered into force on 2nd May 1968 introduced the liberty</p>\n<p>not to be imprisoned for failure to fulfill a contractual obligation, the right not</p>\n<p>to be expelled from a territory of a state of which one is not a national.</p>\n<p>Protocol No. 6, which entered into force on 1 March 1985, inter-alia, provided</p>\n<p>for the abolish ion of the death penalty.</p>\n<p>Protocol No 7, which entered into force on 1 November 1988, provides for the</p>\n<p>expulsion of aliens, after the alien has been given the opportunity to give reasons</p>\n<p>against his expulsion, to have his case reviewed and the right to be represented</p>\n<p>before competent authorities, the right of those convicted of a criminal offence</p>\n<p>to have the sentence reviewed by higher tribunals, the right not to be punished</p>\n<p>twice for the same offence (Double Jeopardy), the right to enjoy equality of rights</p>\n<p>and responsibilities.</p>\n<p>Protocol No 12, which was made on 4 November 2000, which provides for</p>\n<p>general prohibition of discrimination.</p>\n<p>The Convention guarantees the right to life in Article 2; Freedom from torture</p>\n<p>or prohibition of torture in Article 3; Prohibition of slavery and forced labour in</p>\n<p>Article 4; The Right of Liberty and Security in Article 5; The Right to a Fair Trial</p>\n<p>in Article 6; It guarantees that punishment must be in accordance with the law in</p>\n<p>Article 7; It provides for the Right to Respect for Private and Family life in Article</p>\n<p>8; Freedom of Thought, Conscience and Religion in Article 9; Freedom of</p>\n<p>Expression in Article 10; Freedom of Assembly and Association in Article 11;</p>\n<p>the Right to Marry in Article 12;</p>\n<p>The Right to an Effective Remedy in Article 13; Prohibition of Discrimination</p>\n<p>in Article 14; and Derogation in Article 15.</p>\n<p>The European Convention on Human Rights covers civil and political rights.</p>\n<p>The social and economic rights are dealt with in the European Social Charter of</p>\n<h3 id=\"1961-the-social-charter-has-additional-protocols-of-1988-1991-1995-1996-and\">1961. The Social Charter has additional Protocols of 1988, 1991, 1995, 1996 and</h3>\n<p>1999.</p>\n<p>One of the drafters of the European Convention on Human Rights, one Pierre-</p>\n<p>Henri-Teitgen, explained in 1949 why economic and social rights were not</p>\n<h4 id=\"included-in-the-european-convention-he-said\">included in the European Convention. He said:</h4>\n<p>It was first necessary to guarantee democracy in the</p>\n<p>European           Union and then coordinate our economies,</p>\n<p>before undertaking     the generalization of social democracy.46</p>\n<p>3.1.4 Admissibility</p>\n<p>Every individual application is assigned to a Section, whose President designates</p>\n<p>a rapporteur. After a preliminary examination of the case, the rapporteur decides</p>\n<p>whether it should be dealt with by a three-member Committee or by a Chamber.</p>\n<p>A Committee may decide, by unanimous vote, to declare inadmissible or strike</p>\n<p>out an application where it can do so without further examination. Individual</p>\n<p>applications which are not declared inadmissible by Committees, or which are</p>\n<p>referred directly to a Chamber by the rapporteur, and State applications are</p>\n<p>examined by a Chamber. Chambers determine both admissibility and merits, in</p>\n<p>separate decisions or where appropriate together.</p>\n<p>Chambers may at any time relinquish jurisdiction in favour of the Grand</p>\n<p>Chamber where a case raises a serious question of interpretation of the</p>\n<p>Convention or where there is a risk of departing from existing case-law, unless</p>\n<p>one of the parties objects to such relinquishment within one month of</p>\n<p>notification of the intention to relinquish. In the event of relinquishment the</p>\n<p>procedure followed is the same as that set out below for Chambers.</p>\n<p>The first stage of the procedure is generally written, although the Chamber may</p>\n<p>decide to hold a public hearing, in which case issues arising in relation to the</p>\n<p>merits will normally also be addressed.</p>\n<p>Decisions on admissibility, which are taken by majority vote, must contain</p>\n<p>reasons and be made public.</p>\n<p>3.1.5 The European Court of Human Rights</p>\n<p>The European Court of Human Rights set up under the Convention as amended</p>\n<p>by Protocol No. 11 is composed of a number of judges equal to that of the</p>\n<p>46</p>\n<p>See Stein infra p 794.</p>\n<p>Contracting States. The present number is forty-four. It should be noted that</p>\n<p>there is no restriction on the number of judges of the same nationality.</p>\n<p>Judges are elected by the Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe for</p>\n<p>six years. The terms of office of one half of the judges elected at the first election</p>\n<p>expired after three years, so as to ensure that the terms of office of one half of</p>\n<p>the judges are renewed every three years.</p>\n<p>Judges sit on the Court in their individual capacity and do not represent any State.</p>\n<p>They are precluded from engaging in any activity, which is incompatible with</p>\n<p>their independence or impartiality or with the demands of full-time office. Their</p>\n<p>terms of office expire when they reach the age of seventy.</p>\n<p>The Plenary Court elects its President, two Vice-Presidents and two Presidents</p>\n<p>of Section for a period of three years.</p>\n<p>3.1.6 The Divisions of the Court</p>\n<p>Under the Rules of Court, the Court is divided into four Sections, whose</p>\n<p>composition, fixed for three years, is geographically and gender balanced and</p>\n<p>takes account of the different legal systems of the Contracting States. Two of the</p>\n<p>Sections are presided over by the Vice-Presidents of the Court; the other two</p>\n<p>Sections are presided over by the Section Presidents. Section Presidents are</p>\n<p>assisted and where necessary replaced by Section VicePresidents, elected by the</p>\n<p>Sections.</p>\n<p>Committees of three judges are set up within each Section for twelve-month</p>\n<p>periods.</p>\n<p>Chambers of seven members are constituted within each Section on the basis of</p>\n<p>rotation, with the Section President and the judge elected in respect of the State</p>\n<p>concerned sitting in each case. Where the latter is not a member of the Section,</p>\n<p>he or she sits as an ex officio member of the Chamber. The members of the Section</p>\n<p>who are not full members of the Chamber sit as substitute members.</p>\n<p>The Grand Chamber of the Court is composed of seventeen judges, who include,</p>\n<p>as ex officio members, the President, Vice-Presidents and Section Presidents.</p>\n<p>The Court provides for two procedures, by which the member states also</p>\n<p>referred to, as the High Contracting Parties, may be held accountable by the</p>\n<p>European Court of Human Rights, for any violation of the rights contained in</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-convention-these-procedures-are-discussed-below\">the Convention. These procedures are discussed below:</h4>\n<h4 id=\"3-1-7-the-procedure-of-the-court\">3.1.7 The Procedure of the Court:</h4>\n<p>Any Contracting State (State application) or individual claiming to be a victim of</p>\n<p>a violation of the Convention (individual application) may lodge directly with the</p>\n<p>Court in Strasbourg an application alleging a breach by a Contracting State of</p>\n<p>one of the Convention rights. A notice for the guidance of applicants and forms</p>\n<p>for making applications may be obtained from the Registry. The Individual</p>\n<p>Petition Procedure provided for under Article 34. The other is an Inter State</p>\n<p>Procedure provided for under Article 33.</p>\n<p>The procedure before the European Court of Human Rights is adversarial and</p>\n<p>public. Hearings, which are held only in a minority of cases, are public, unless</p>\n<p>the Chamber/Grand Chamber decides otherwise on account of exceptional</p>\n<p>circumstances. Memorials and other documents filed with the</p>\n<p>Court’s Registry by the parties are, in principle, accessible to the public.</p>\n<p>Individual applicants may submit applications themselves, but legal</p>\n<p>representation is recommended, and even required for hearings or once an</p>\n<p>application has been declared admissible. The Council of Europe has set up a</p>\n<p>legal aid scheme for applicants who do not have sufficient means.</p>\n<p>The official languages of the Court are English and French, but applications may</p>\n<p>be submitted in one of the official languages of the Contracting States. Once the</p>\n<p>application has been declared admissible, one of the Court’s official languages</p>\n<p>must be used, unless the President of the Chamber/Grand Chamber authorises</p>\n<p>the continued use of the language of the application.</p>\n<h4 id=\"3-1-8-the-operation-of-the-court\">3.1.8 The Operation of The Court:</h4>\n<p>The operation of the Court and the entire European System underwent changes</p>\n<p>in November 1998, when Protocol No. 11 to the European Convention entered</p>\n<p>into force.</p>\n<p>Under the old system the main stage in the examination of the a complaint was</p>\n<p>undertaken by the European Commission of Human Rights. The Commission</p>\n<p>ceased to exist in October 1999 after the merger or fusion of the old court and</p>\n<p>the Commission.47</p>\n<p>Prior to unification the Commission comprised an expert elected from every</p>\n<p>state party to the Convention. The findings of the Commission on whether the</p>\n<p>Convention was breached or not, was not per se’ legally binding on the states</p>\n<p>parties. It was also the practice that within 3 months of the Commission’s report</p>\n<p>pertaining to the case, the case could have been referred to the Court by either</p>\n<p>the Commission or by the state party concerned, but not the complainant.</p>\n<p>Under the old system if the case did not reach the Court, the final decision was</p>\n<p>made by the Committee of Ministers. In a way the Old Court was at the mercy</p>\n<p>of the Commission or the state concerned.</p>\n<p>3.1.9 Procedure on the Merits</p>\n<p>Once the Chamber has decided to admit the application, it may invite the parties</p>\n<p>to submit further evidence and written observations, including any claims for</p>\n<p>&quot;just satisfaction&quot; by the applicant. If no hearing has taken place at the</p>\n<p>admissibility stage, it may decide to hold a hearing on the merits of the case.</p>\n<p>The President of the Chamber may, in the interests of the proper administration</p>\n<p>of justice, invite or grant leave to any Contracting State which is not party to the</p>\n<p>proceedings, or any person concerned who is not the applicant, to submit written</p>\n<p>comments, and, in exceptional circumstances, to make representations at the</p>\n<p>hearing. A Contracting State whose national is an applicant in the case is entitled</p>\n<p>to intervene as of right.</p>\n<p>During the procedure on the merits, negotiations aimed at securing a friendly</p>\n<p>settlement may be conducted through the Registrar. The negotiations are</p>\n<p>confidential.</p>\n<p>3.1.10 Judgments</p>\n<p>47</p>\n<p>See Stein supra p………………..</p>\n<p>Chambers decide by a majority vote. Any judge who has taken part in the</p>\n<p>consideration of the case is entitled to append to the judgment a separate</p>\n<p>opinion, either concurring or dissenting, or a bare statement of dissent.</p>\n<p>Within three months of delivery of the judgment of a Chamber, any party may</p>\n<p>request that the case be referred to the Grand Chamber if it raises a serious</p>\n<p>question of interpretation or application or a serious issue of general importance.</p>\n<p>Such requests are examined by a Grand Chamber panel of five judges composed</p>\n<p>of the President of the Court, the Section Presidents, with the exception of the</p>\n<p>Section President who presides over the Section to which the Chamber that gave</p>\n<p>judgment belongs, and another judge selected by rotation from judges who were</p>\n<p>not members of the original Chamber.</p>\n<p>A Chamber’s judgment becomes final on expiry of the three-month period or</p>\n<p>earlier if the parties announce that they have no intention of requesting a referral</p>\n<p>or after a decision of the panel rejecting a request for referral.</p>\n<p>If the panel accepts the request, the Grand Chamber renders its decision on the</p>\n<p>case in the form of a judgment. The Grand Chamber decides by a majority vote</p>\n<p>and its judgments are final.</p>\n<p>All final judgments of the Court are binding on the respondent States concerned.</p>\n<p>Responsibility for supervising the execution of judgments lies with the</p>\n<p>Committee of Ministers of the Council of Europe. The Committee of Ministers</p>\n<p>verifies whether States in respect of which a violation of the Convention is found</p>\n<p>have taken adequate remedial measures to comply with the specific or general</p>\n<p>obligations arising out of the Court’s judgments.</p>\n<p>3.1.11 Advisory opinions</p>\n<p>The Court may, at the request of the Committee of Ministers, give advisory</p>\n<p>opinions on legal questions concerning the interpretation of the Convention and</p>\n<p>Protocols. Decisions of the Committee of Ministers to request an advisory</p>\n<p>opinion are taken by a majority vote. Advisory opinions are given by the Grand</p>\n<p>Chamber and adopted by a majority vote. Any judge may attach to the advisory</p>\n<p>opinion, a separate opinion or a bare statement of dissent.</p>\n<p>3.2 The Human Rights System Under the American Convention on</p>\n<p>Human Rights</p>\n<p>The Convention gives authority to supervise observance of the Convention to</p>\n<p>two organs. These are The Inter American Commission, which was in existence</p>\n<p>before the Convention and the Inter American Court of Human Rights, which</p>\n<p>was created by the Convention.</p>\n<h4 id=\"3-2-3-the-inter-american-system\">3.2.3 The Inter-American System:</h4>\n<p>In May 1948 an Inter-American conference was held in Bogota</p>\n<p>Columbia. This was the ninth Conference. It was at this conference that the</p>\n<p>Organization of American States (OAS) was established. At the end of this</p>\n<p>conference the OAS Charter was made and it entered into force in 1951. The</p>\n<p>OAS was preceded by the International Union of American Republics 1890.</p>\n<p>The OAS Charter has been amended by many protocols, including the Protocol</p>\n<p>of Buenos Aires of 1967, the Protocol of Cartagena de Indias of 1985, the</p>\n<p>Protocol of Washington 1992, and the Protocol of Managua of 1993.</p>\n<p>3.2.4 The Purposes of the OAS</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-purposes-of-oas-are-as-follows\">The purposes of OAS are as follows:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-to-strengthen-the-peace-and-security-of-the-continent\">1. To strengthen the peace and security of the continent;</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-to-promote-and-consolidate-representative-democracy-with-respect-for\">2. To promote and consolidate representative democracy, with respect for</h3>\n<p>the principle of nonintervention;</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-to-prevent-possible-causes-of-difficulties-that-may-arise-among-member\">3. To prevent possible causes of difficulties that may arise among member</h3>\n<p>states in the event of aggression;</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-to-seek-the-solution-of-political-judicial-and-economic-problems-that-may\">4. To seek the solution of political, judicial and economic problems that may</h3>\n<p>arise among them;</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-to-promote-by-cooperative-action-their-economic-social-and-cultural\">5. To promote by cooperative action, their economic, social and cultural</h3>\n<p>development</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-to-achieve-an-effective-limitation-of-conventional-weapons-that-will-make\">6. To achieve an effective limitation of conventional weapons that will make</h3>\n<p>it possible to devote the largest amount of resources to the economic and</p>\n<p>social development of the member states.48</p>\n<p>3.2.5 The Structure of the OAS</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-oas-has-four-main-organs-these-are\">The OAS has four main organs. These are:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-general-assembly-which-meets-annually-and-in-addition-special\">1. The General Assembly, which meets annually and in addition special</h3>\n<p>sessions as and when required.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-meeting-of-consultation-of-ministers-of-foreign-affairs-that\">2. The meeting of Consultation of Ministers of Foreign Affairs that</h3>\n<p>considers urgent matters;</p>\n<p>48</p>\n<p>See Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission of Human Rights 1994 (1995) at 347.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-permanent-council-and\">3. The Permanent Council; and</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-general-secretariat\">4. The General Secretariat.</h3>\n<p>The last two are based in Washington District of Columbia.</p>\n<p>3.2.6 The Inter American Commission on Human Rights</p>\n<p>The Inter American Commission comprises 7 members elected in a</p>\n<p>nongovernmental capacity by the OAS Assembly, and represent all OAS</p>\n<p>member states.</p>\n<p>The unique character of the Inter American Commission is that when the</p>\n<p>Convention entered into force in 1978, the Commission got a second role. The</p>\n<p>first one is that it still remains an organ of the OAS, which role permits it to</p>\n<p>maintain its power to promote and protect human rights in the territories of all</p>\n<p>OAS member states. The second role is that it is now an organ of the</p>\n<p>Convention, with capacity to supervise human rights issues in the territory of the</p>\n<p>parties to the Convention.</p>\n<p>The Commission s function include</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-promoting-human-rights-in-all-oas-member-states\">1. Promoting human rights in all OAS member states,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-assisting-in-drafting-of-human-rights-documents\">2. Assisting in drafting of human rights documents,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-advising-member-states-of-oas\">3. Advising member states of OAS,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-preparing-country-reports\">4. Preparing country reports,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-mediating-disputes-over-serious-human-rights-problems\">5. Mediating disputes over serious human rights problems,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"6-handling-individual-complaints-and-initiating-individual-cases-on-its-own\">6. handling individual complaints and initiating individual cases on its own</h3>\n<p>motion, both with regard to states parties and states not parties to the</p>\n<p>Convention, and</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-participating-in-the-handling-of-cases-and-advisory-opinions-before-the\">7. Participating in the handling of cases and advisory opinions before the</h3>\n<p>Court.</p>\n<p>3.2.7 The Inter American Court</p>\n<p>The Inter American Court consists of 7 judges, irrespective of the number of</p>\n<p>states that have recognized the jurisdiction of the Court. Judges however may</p>\n<p>come from countries of the OAS, which have not become parties to the</p>\n<p>Convention, e.g Judges from the United States of America. The court has both</p>\n<p>advisory opinion jurisdiction and contentious or adjudicative jurisdiction.</p>\n<p>3.2.8 The Human Rights System Under the American Convention on</p>\n<p>Human Rights</p>\n<p>The above-mentioned Bogota conference of May 1948 also adopted the</p>\n<p>American Declaration of the Rights and Duties of Man. This means that the</p>\n<p>Inter American system had a human rights declaration prior to the United</p>\n<p>Nations. (Seven months to be precise, because the UDHR was proclaimed by</p>\n<p>the U.N in December 1948.</p>\n<p>The Rights contained in the American Declaration of Rights and Duties of</p>\n<p>Man are similar to those contained in the Universal Declaration of Human</p>\n<p>Rights. However, the former has 10 distinct articles setting out the duties of</p>\n<p>citizens, the duty to conduct himself in relation to others that each and every one</p>\n<p>may fully form and develop his personality; to ‘aid support, educate and protect</p>\n<p>his minor children to acquire at least an elementary education’, to vote in popular</p>\n<p>elections, to obey the law and other legitimate commands of the authorities, to</p>\n<p>render whatever civil and military service his country may require for its defense</p>\n<p>and preservation, to cooperate with states with respect to social security and</p>\n<p>welfare; and to pay taxes and work..49</p>\n<p>As of March 2000 the OAS had 35 member states of which 25 had ratified the</p>\n<p>American Convention and 20 had recognized the jurisdiction of the Court. Peru</p>\n<p>in the recent past renounced the jurisdiction of the Court.</p>\n<p>The United States of America signed the Convention in 1978, but up to date</p>\n<p>(June 2003) not ratified it.</p>\n<p>49</p>\n<p>See Henry J. Steiner and Philip Alston: International Human Rights in Context, Law Politics and Morals,</p>\n<p>Second Edition, Oxford University Press, 2000, p870.</p>\n<p>Cuba is still a member, but the government of Fidel Castro has been excluded</p>\n<p>from participating in the work of the OAS. Despite this exclusion, the OAS has</p>\n<p>continued to examine the human rights record of Cuba.</p>\n<p>Although the institutional structures of the Inter-American system are similar to</p>\n<p>their European counterpart, the development of the InterAmerican system has</p>\n<p>followed a different course all together. While authoritarian and military</p>\n<p>governments were rare in Europe, they were not uncommon in the Latin</p>\n<p>American context in the 1980.</p>\n<p>The Convention gives authority to supervise observance of the Convention to</p>\n<p>two organs. These are: The Inter-American Commission on Human Rights,</p>\n<p>which was in existence before the Convention came into being; and the Inter</p>\n<p>American Court on Human Rights, which was created by the Convention.</p>\n<p>3.3 The African System.50</p>\n<p>The African Union (AU) formerly known as the Organization of African Unity</p>\n<p>(OAU) also came up with a regional human rights instrument. The instrument</p>\n<p>was called the African Charter on Human and People’s Rights 1981.</p>\n<p>50</p>\n<p>See also: Robert H.Kisanga: ‘Fundamental Rights and Freedoms in Africa: The Work of the African</p>\n<p>Commission on Human and People’s Rights’ in Chris Maina Peter and Ibrahim Hamis Juma (editors)</p>\n<p>Fundamental Rights and Freedoms in Tanzania, Mkuki Nyota Publishers, Dar Es Salaam, Tanzania, 1998,</p>\n<p>pp. 25 – 35.</p>\n<p>The very first discussion, which later culminated in the Charter began as early as</p>\n<p>1961, during the African Conference on the rule of law in Lagos, Nigeria,</p>\n<p>convened by the International Commission of Jurists. The efforts were later</p>\n<p>boosted in 1963, when the then independent African states came together and in</p>\n<p>their founding Charter made reference to human rights, but their concern was</p>\n<p>the total removal of colonialism and apartheid.51</p>\n<p>From 28 November to 8 December 1979, a Ministerial conference was convened</p>\n<p>by the O.A.U. Assembly, which met in Dakar, Senegal. It was attended by</p>\n<p>Ministers of Justice and a number of African experts. This gathering was</p>\n<p>responsible for the drafting of the African Charter on Human and People Rights,</p>\n<p>also known as the Banjul Charter. Further meetings were held in Banjul in June</p>\n<p>1980 and January 1981 as we will see later here below.</p>\n<p>As a result of those initiatives, the OAU as it was known at the time, passed a</p>\n<p>resolution, which called upon the Secretary General to constitute a group of</p>\n<p>experts to prepare a draft Charter. After a thorough job was done, the draft was</p>\n<p>then passed to the Ministers of Justice of the OAU. The Charter was adopted by</p>\n<p>the Heads of State and Government during an OAU summit that was held in</p>\n<p>Nairobi, Kenya. There was initially a contentious debate as to whether the</p>\n<p>Charter should be called the Banjul Charter, given Banjul’s role as a venue</p>\n<p>where a lot of work pertaining to the drafting and preparation of the Charter</p>\n<p>took place, or the Nairobi Charter, because the Charter was finally adopted in</p>\n<p>Nairobi. The OAU finally decided to settle for a neutral name, hence the African</p>\n<p>Charter on Human and People’s Rights.</p>\n<p>In preparing the draft Charter the African experts were mandated to prepare a</p>\n<p>human rights instrument based on African legal philosophy, responsive to</p>\n<p>African aspirations and needs.52 This then meant that the Charter was to address</p>\n<p>African aspirations and needs.53 The African jurists ensured that the instrument</p>\n<p>51</p>\n<p>Gittelman, R. ‘The African Charter on Human and People’s Rights Analysis’, 22 Virginian Journal of</p>\n<p>International Law (1982) Vol. 4, p670.</p>\n<p>52</p>\n<p>Gittelman, R. ‘Introductory Note: Organization of African Unity: The Banjul Charter on Human and</p>\n<p>People’s Rights,’ International Legal Material (ILM) 21 (1982) 58.</p>\n<p>53</p>\n<p>Gittelman, R. ‘Introductory Note: Organization of African Unity: The Banjul Charter on Human and</p>\n<p>People’s Rights,’ International Legal Material (ILM) 21 (1982) 58. See also Kasozi George W.K.L. ‘How</p>\n<p>well suited is the Banjul Charter to Promote and Protect Human Rights as compared with other International</p>\n<p>suitable for the African continent should reflect the philosophical orientation and</p>\n<p>disposition of the African values the continent hold, as suggested. As to whether</p>\n<p>there is a common African value shared by the whole continent that is another</p>\n<p>matter, which may be examined. We should also mention here that the preamble</p>\n<p>to the Charter tells a different story when it alludes to the fact that there should</p>\n<p>be a link between the universality of human rights and the African customs and</p>\n<h4 id=\"tradition-the-preamble-provides-inter-alia\">tradition. The preamble provides inter alia:</h4>\n<p>…Reaffirming the pledge they solemnly made in Article 2 of the said</p>\n<p>Charter to eradicate all forms of colonialism from Africa,</p>\n<p>to coordinate and intensify their cooperation and efforts to achieve a</p>\n<p>better life for the peoples of Africa and to promote international</p>\n<p>cooperation having due regard to the Charter of the United Nations and</p>\n<p>the Universal Declaration of Human Rights;</p>\n<p>Taking into consideration the virtues of their historical tradition and the</p>\n<p>values of African civilization which should inspire and characterize their</p>\n<p>Reflection on the concept of human and people’s rights;</p>\n<p>In June 1981 the O.A.U. adopted unanimously the African Charter on Human</p>\n<p>and Peoples Rights. This was during the eighteenth Assembly of Heads of State</p>\n<p>and Government of the O.A.U. The Charter took two years to draft, and its</p>\n<p>adoption was seen as an historic step towards the protection of human rights in</p>\n<p>Africa.</p>\n<p>It has been observed by Prof. Rembe that the adoption of the African Charter</p>\n<p>was a result of a series of efforts and pressures generated from within and outside</p>\n<p>Africa, for the promotion and protection of human rights.54</p>\n<p>Instruments’. Essay No.3, Submitted to the University of Hull England, UK for partial requirements of LL.M</p>\n<p>Degree, 1987.</p>\n<p>54</p>\n<p>Rembe NS, The System of Protection of Human Rights inder the African Charter on Human and</p>\n<p>People’s Rights; Problems and Prospects, Human and People’s Rights Monograph Series No. 6 Institute of</p>\n<p>Southern Africa Studies (ISAS) – National University of Lesotho (NUL) Roma, Lesotho, 1991 p1.</p>\n<p>The Charter entered into force on 21st October 1986 after the required number</p>\n<p>of states ratified it.55 Due to time constraints it was not possible to get data on</p>\n<p>ratification status. The students should get the data on their own.</p>\n<p>The legal basis for the African Charter on Human and People’s Rights is</p>\n<p>contained in article II (1) (b) of the Charter of the Organization of African Unity,</p>\n<h4 id=\"which-obliges-member-states-to\">which obliges member states to:</h4>\n<p>Coordinate and intensify their efforts to achieve a</p>\n<p>Better life for the people of Africa56.</p>\n<h4 id=\"3-4-the-charter-and-rights-protected\">3.4 The Charter and Rights Protected:</h4>\n<p>The Charter, which is said to be the newest, least developed or effective, in</p>\n<p>relation to the European and the Inter American regimes57, guarantees many civil,</p>\n<p>political and economic rights, including the right to life, the right not to be</p>\n<p>tortured, freedom from arbitrary arrest and detention, the right to a fair trial, the</p>\n<p>right of equal protection of the law, freedom of expression, association and</p>\n<p>assembly.</p>\n<p>It also provides for collective rights characterized as people’s rights, such as the</p>\n<p>right to self-determination, the right to a clean environment, and the right to</p>\n<p>development.</p>\n<p>The Charter also imposes duties on the people as well as states within the</p>\n<p>jurisdiction of the Charter. These include the obligation to respect one’s parents</p>\n<p>and to help them in case of need, and the obligation to strive at all times to</p>\n<p>promote and achieve African unity. As far as states are concerned, they are</p>\n<p>enjoined to assist the family to uphold and promote morals and traditional values</p>\n<p>of the community and to guarantee the right to development58.</p>\n<h4 id=\"3-5-the-institutional-structure-of-the-african-system\">3.5 The Institutional Structure of the African System:</h4>\n<p>55</p>\n<p>Find out the status of ratification of the African Charter.</p>\n<p>56</p>\n<p>See also Henry J Steiner and Philip Alston: International Human Rights in Context: Law Politics,</p>\n<p>Morals, Oxford University Press, Second Edition, p 921.</p>\n<p>57</p>\n<p>Ibid p 920.</p>\n<p>58</p>\n<p>See Kisanga supra, p 27.</p>\n<p>3.5.1 The Commission on Human and People’s Rights</p>\n<p>The African Commission on Human and People’s Rights, compared to the Inter</p>\n<p>American Commission and the European Commission of Human Rights, (until</p>\n<p>it was merged with the European Court of Human Rights in 19…..), is relatively</p>\n<p>new. It was established in 1987, thus as of June 2003, it has had only 15 years of</p>\n<p>experience.</p>\n<p>The Commission is established in terms of Article 31 of the African Charter on</p>\n<h4 id=\"human-and-people-s-rights-it-states-that\">Human and People’s Rights. It states that:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-commission-shall-consist-of-eleven-members-chosen-from\">1. The Commission shall consist of eleven members chosen from</h3>\n<p>amongst</p>\n<p>African personalities of the highest reputation, known for their high</p>\n<p>morality, integrity, impartiality and competence in matters of human</p>\n<p>and</p>\n<p>People’s Rights, particular consideration being given to persons having</p>\n<p>legal experience.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-members-of-the-commission-shall-serve-in-their-personal\">2. The members of the Commission shall serve in their personal</h3>\n<p>capacities.</p>\n<p>The mandate of the Commission is contained in Article 45, which provides as</p>\n<p>follows.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-functions-of-the-commission-shall-be\">The functions of the Commission shall be:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-to-promote-human-and-people-s-rights-and-in-particular\">1. To promote Human and People’s Rights and in particular:</h3>\n<p>(a) to collect documents, undertake studies and researches on</p>\n<p>African</p>\n<p>problems in the field of human and people’s rights, organize</p>\n<p>seminars,</p>\n<p>symposia, and conferences, disseminate information,</p>\n<p>encourage national</p>\n<p>and local institutions concerned with human and people’s</p>\n<p>rights, and</p>\n<p>should the case arise, give its views or make recommendations</p>\n<p>to Governments.</p>\n<p>(b) to formulate and lay down, principles and rules aimed at solving</p>\n<p>legal</p>\n<p>problems relating to human and people’s rights and</p>\n<p>fundamental</p>\n<p>freedoms upon which African Governments may base their</p>\n<p>legislation</p>\n<p>(c) cooperate with other African and international institutions</p>\n<p>concerned</p>\n<p>with the promotion and protection of human and people’s</p>\n<p>rights.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-ensure-the-protection-of-human-and-people-s-rights-under-conditions\">2. Ensure the protection of human and people’s rights under conditions</h3>\n<p>laid down In the Charter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-interpret-all-the-provisions-of-the-present-charter-at-the-request-of-a\">3. Interpret all the provisions of the present Charter at the request of a</h3>\n<p>state party,</p>\n<p>Institutions of the OAU or an African Organization recognized by the</p>\n<h2 id=\"oau\">OAU.</h2>\n<h3 id=\"4-perform-any-other-task-which-may-be-entrusted-to-it-by-the-assembly\">4. Perform any other task which may be entrusted to it by the Assembly</h3>\n<p>of Heads of State and Government.</p>\n<p>3.5.2 Formation of the African Court on Human and Peoples Rights</p>\n<p>3.5.3 Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa</p>\n<p>The African Charter on Human and People’s Rights, (ACHPR) 1981</p>\n<p>guarantees enjoyment of human rights.</p>\n<p>Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa, adopted on 11th July 2003, by</p>\n<p>the</p>\n<p>African Union. This protocol supplements the ACHPR)</p>\n<p>It is the first Protocol to address women’s rights including reproductive</p>\n<p>rights</p>\n<p>Test It is said to be a groundbreaking Protocol, for the first time in</p>\n<p>international law, explicitly sets forth the reproductive right of women to</p>\n<p>medical abortion when pregnancy results from rape or incest or when the</p>\n<p>continuation of pregnancy endangers the health or life of the mother.</p>\n<p>The Protocol also explicitly calls for the legal prohibition of female genital</p>\n<p>mutilation, and prohibits the abuse of women in advertising and</p>\n<p>pornography. The Protocol sets forth a broad range of economic and social</p>\n<p>welfare rights for women. The rights of particularly vulnerable groups of</p>\n<p>women, including widows, elderly women, disabled women and “women in</p>\n<p>distress,” which includes poor women, women from marginalized</p>\n<p>populations groups, and pregnant or nursing women in detention are</p>\n<p>specifically recognized</p>\n<p>Status of Ratification of the Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa</p>\n<p>The countries that have ratified the Protocol as of 26 February 2008 are</p>\n<h4 id=\"twenty-three\">twenty-three:</h4>\n<p>Angola, Benin, Burkina Faso, Cape Verde, The Comoros, Djibouti, The</p>\n<p>Gambia, Ghana, Lesotho, Libya, Malawi, Mali, Mauritania, Mozambique,</p>\n<p>Namibia, Nigeria, Rwanda, Senegal, Seychelles, South Africa, Tanzania, Togo</p>\n<p>and Zambia.</p>\n<p>• Uganda: Abortion Complicates Ratification of Protocol on Women&#x27;s</p>\n<p>Rights</p>\n<p>• TheMonitor       (Kampala) 31     October      2007</p>\n<p>Posted to the web 30 October 2007</p>\n<p>Kakaire A. Kirunda</p>\n<p>The government is under renewed pressure to embrace the protocol to the</p>\n<p>African charter on human and peoples&#x27; rights on the rights of women in</p>\n<p>Africa, also known as the Maputo Protocol.</p>\n<p>Women activists demanded ratification of the protocol at a recent workshop</p>\n<p>in Kampala. The protocol, which came into force in October 2005, covers a</p>\n<p>broad range of women&#x27;s rights ranging from the elimination of discrimination</p>\n<p>against women to health and reproductive rights. However, Article 14 of the</p>\n<p>protocol that calls for the legalisation of medical abortion especially in cases</p>\n<p>of sexual assault, rape and incest has drawn criticism from religious leaders.</p>\n<p>Reproductive rights activists have since argued that the continued failure by</p>\n<p>several Africa Union Governments has greatly contributed to maternal</p>\n<p>mortality. In Uganda alone, maternal mortality stands at close to 500 per</p>\n<p>100,000 live births.</p>\n<p>And an estimated 300,000 abortions (most of them unsafe) are carried out</p>\n<p>every year. Although the protocol espouses the right to life, its support for</p>\n<p>&quot;reproductive rights&quot; has been interpreted as a nod of approval to abortion.</p>\n<p>Soon after its adoption by the 2nd ordinary session of the Assembly of the</p>\n<p>African Union in July 2003, opposition to the protocol started and has never</p>\n<p>relented. The onslaught is led by Catholic bishops.</p>\n<h4 id=\"quot-this-protocol-is-the-slow-but-sure-destruction-of-fundamental-african-values\">&quot;This Protocol is the slow but sure destruction of fundamental African values:</h4>\n<p>Respect for life, the importance of the family, motherhood, fertility and</p>\n<p>marriage. All of these values are ignored by the Protocol,&quot; allafrica.com</p>\n<p>quoted the former Guinea Conakry Archbishop Robert Sarah at the end of</p>\n<p>the Vatican&#x27;s 15th missiology meeting in early July.</p>\n<p>Her message has been echoed by counterparts from Kenya, Burundi, DR</p>\n<p>Congo, Tanzania, Mozambique among others. The Uganda Joint Christian</p>\n<p>Council (UJCC) last year sought intervention from President Yoweri</p>\n<p>Museveni.</p>\n<p>&quot;We request President Museveni and the delegation that will represent</p>\n<p>Uganda at the upcoming meeting of the African Union in Addis Abba to</p>\n<p>reject any policy that would expose Uganda in particular, and Africa as a</p>\n<p>whole, to mass murder through the legalisation of abortion,&quot; UJCC&#x27;s Deputy</p>\n<p>Secretary for Finance and Administration, Sylvester Arinaitwe wrote</p>\n<p>Since October 26, 2005, 22 countries have ratified the Protocol. But why is</p>\n<p>Uganda not among these countries? Is it that the religious leaders have</p>\n<p>emerged the victors? Or is it that since the Uganda Constitution has been</p>\n<p>hailed as one of the most progressive the world over, it takes care of the</p>\n<p>Maputo protocol requirements and there is no need to domesticate the latter.</p>\n<p>In a recent interview, the State Minister for Gender, Ms Rukia Nakadama</p>\n<p>Isanga said while Uganda was yet to ratify the Maputo protocol, most of its</p>\n<p>requirements have been answered through various policies in place.</p>\n<p>&quot;You may not notice it but for every issue raised in each of the articles of the</p>\n<p>protocol we have a policy in place that is gender sensitive. The Constitution</p>\n<p>too ably addresses most of these issues.</p>\n<p>&quot;But this does not mean that Uganda will not accept the protocol. These</p>\n<p>issues shall be presented to Parliament, debated and a decision will be made</p>\n<p>and there is nothing that shall then stop us from appending our signature,&quot;</p>\n<p>said Isanga.</p>\n<p>On the clergy&#x27;s concerns, Isanga said, &quot;Their main problem is abortion. And</p>\n<p>remember we are a God fearing country as reflected in our motto (For God</p>\n<p>and My Country). But I must also say that no one would want to see women</p>\n<p>dying as a result of botched up abortions. We need to devise ways of</p>\n<p>preventing scenarios that may lead to unwanted pregnancies. We need to</p>\n<p>rebuild the moral fibre.“</p>";
